\"The current frontend stops its PC and discards each fetch opportunity whenever\\na data-memory transaction stalls the in-order pipeline.  The independent\\ninstruction and data buses in the benchmark permit useful instruction fetch\\nduring those holds, creating a reserve that can absorb later random imem\\nbackpressure without changing architectural issue order.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF-to-ID handoff with a compact two-entry elastic\\nfetch queue.  Fetch continues into an empty queue slot while MEM is held for\\ndmem backpressure, and ID consumes queued entries only when it can advance.\\nEach entry carries PC, instruction, and the existing predicted successor;\\nan EX redirect flushes all speculative entries and restarts fetch at the\\nresolved target.  This preserves the proven static prediction policy and\\nsingle-retire pipeline while overlapping independent imem service with data\\nstalls, improving CoreMark IPC in the random-stall cosimulation.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a two-entry valid/ready fetch queue that records PC, instruction, and predicted successor, permits fetch during downstream holds, and flushes precisely on EX redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the direct IF/ID handoff wiring with fetch enqueue/dequeue control and preserve redirect-over-stall priority at the frontend boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate frontend queue consumption from dmem pipeline holding so a full or flushed queue backpressures fetch without allowing younger instructions into held execute state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume only a valid queue head when ID/EX can advance, retaining bubble, flush, and RVFI source-observation semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and 