{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"predictor\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.fitness // \\\"\\\"),(.delta_pct // \\\"\\\"),(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260710-003-r1s2\tStatic loop branch prediction\timprovement\t310.0\t9.61\tAdd a combinational static BTFNT predictor in IF: predict direct JALs and\\nbackward conditional branches taken, using their encoded PC-relative\\nimmediate, and otherwise predict fall-through. Carry the prediction with\\nthe instruction and have EX redirect and flush only on a misprediction;\\nJALR remains fall-through predicted. Correct loop-back predictions admit\\nthe target instruction into decode instead of paying the current recovery\\nbubble, while avoiding the LUT and timing cost of a BTB or dynamic table.\\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r2s2\tReturn-address stack prediction\tregression\t284.75\t-8.15\tAdd a compact four-entry return-address stack. Decode pushes PC+4 for\\naccepted link-register calls and pops accepted canonical returns; IF uses\\nthe stack top to predict JALR x0, 0(x1/x5) as a return target. Keep the\\nexisting BTFNT/JAL policy for all other control flow and retain EX's\\npredicted-versus-resolved next-PC comparison as the single recovery path.\\nUpdating only accepted decode instructions prevents wrong-path calls or\\nreturns from corrupting predictor state while removing routine return\\nbubbles from the timed CoreMark call graph.\\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r3s2\tTiny BTFNT-seeded branch direction table\tregression\t193.29\t-40.29\tAdd a small direct-mapped, two-bit conditional-branch direction table that\\noverrides BTFNT only after that PC has been observed. On a cold entry, IF\\nkeeps the existing backward-taken/forward-not-taken decision and continues\\nto derive the target solely from the instruction immediate. EX trains the\\ntable with the resolved conditional-branch outcome exactly once when the\\ninstruction advances. This learns stable forward and atypical branch bias\\nwhile preserving the existing narrow fetch target path, direct-JAL policy,\\nrecovery mechanism, and precise behavior under memory stalls.\\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r4s2\tOpcode-biased static loop prediction\timprovement\t365.65\t1.14\tRefine the existing stateless BTFNT predictor into an opcode-biased static\\npredictor.  Keep direct JAL prediction unchanged, and predict a backward\\nconditional branch taken only for the loop-continuation predicates\\nBNE, BLT, and BLTU; predict BEQ, BGE, and BGEU fall-through regardless of\\ndisplacement.  The predictor still derives targets solely from the current\\ninstruction immediate and uses the existing EX next-PC comparison for all\\nrecovery, so it adds neither tables nor predictor-state timing while\\navoiding systematic static-direction mistakes on inverted loop exits.\\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r5s2\tProfiled backward range-loop prediction\tregression\t352.87\t-3.5\tExtend the existing stateless backward-loop predicate to include BGE and\\nBGEU, while retaining fall-through prediction for BEQ and preserving the\\ndirect-JAL path. This is a fetch-local funct3 comparison change: targets are\\nstill formed only from the encoded immediate and EX remains the sole\\narchitectural resolver. It recovers the profiled range-loop back edges\\nwithout adding predictor state, tag RAMs, training logic, or another wide\\nnext-PC mux to the timing-critical fetch path.\\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r6s2\tShared static predictor target adder\tregression\t337.65\t-7.66\tPreserve the current direct-JAL and opcode-biased backward BNE/BLT/BLTU\\nprediction policy, but first select a single predicted displacement\\n(four, JAL immediate, or branch immediate) and then compute the successor\\nwith one shared PC adder. This retains identical predicted PCs, EX\\nrecovery, and CoreMark IPC while reducing LUT use and routing pressure in\\nthe timing-sensitive fetch predictor; unlike the failed BHT and RAS, it\\nadds no predictor state, tags, or target mux after a wide arithmetic cone.\\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.id == \\\"hyp-20260710-002-r4s1\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260710-003-r3s2\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nCATEGORY: \\\\(.category)\\\\nOUTCOME: \\\\(.outcome) fitness=\\\\(.fitness) delta=\\\\(.delta_pct)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && sed -n '1,160p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && ls cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses | tail -20\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.id == \\\"hyp-20260710-002-r4s1\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260710-003-r3s2\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nCATEGORY: \\\\(.category)\\\\nOUTCOME: \\\\(.outcome) fitness=\\\\(.fitness) delta=\\\\(.delta_pct)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && sed -n '1,160p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && ls cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses | tail -20\"","aggregated_output":"ID: hyp-20260710-003-r3s2\nTITLE: Tiny BTFNT-seeded branch direction table\nCATEGORY: predictor\nOUTCOME: regression fitness=193.29 delta=-40.29\nHYPOTHESIS:\nAdd a small direct-mapped, two-bit conditional-branch direction table that\noverrides BTFNT only after that PC has been observed. On a cold entry, IF\nkeeps the existing backward-taken/forward-not-taken decision and continues\nto derive the target solely from the instruction immediate. EX trains the\ntable with the resolved conditional-branch outcome exactly once when the\ninstruction advances. This learns stable forward and atypical branch bias\nwhile preserving the existing narrow fetch target path, direct-JAL policy,\nrecovery mechanism, and precise behavior under memory stalls.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\nID: hyp-20260710-002-r4s1\nTITLE: Decode-stage JALR recovery\nCATEGORY: structural\nOUTCOME: regression fitness=324.04 delta=-10.37\nHYPOTHESIS:\nResolve dependency-free, aligned legal JALRs in ID using the existing\nwrite-first regfile value and redirect fetch at the ID/EX boundary. Record\nthe resolved target as that instruction's predicted successor so its normal\nEX pass does not redirect again. If the immediately older EX instruction\nwrites JALR's rs1, or the target is misaligned, defer to the present EX\nresolver and trap path. EX redirects retain priority over an ID redirect,\nensuring a wrong-path JALR is flushed. This removes one recovery cycle for\nordinary returns without widening the IF prediction datapath or changing\nretirement order.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (improvement, +9.61%): Carrying IF's predicted next PC to EX lets a static BTFNT policy avoid recovery bubbles on CoreMark's frequent backward counted-loop branches (and direct JALs), making low-cost fetch-side control prediction an effective IPC optimization without a BTB.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): A zero-bubble MEM-to-EX load bypass must be qualified by the actual memory-response handshake (and freeze the consumer until then), since selecting combinational load data before acceptance corrupts dependent register results under dmem backpressure.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +4.42%): Once the MEM-to-EX load bypass is response-qualified and the pipeline holds on dmem backpressure, removing the load-use interlock recovers IPC on CoreMark's adjacent pointer-chasing and state-update load consumers without weakening in-order correctness.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, -8.15%): A RAS-top mux and canonical-return decode on the IF next-PC path can cost more timing than CoreMark gains from avoiding return recoveries, so return prediction must preserve the fetch critical path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (improvement, +11.68%): Feeding opcode-selected sign/zero-extended 33-bit operands into one signed multiplier preserves all RV32M high-half products while eliminating parallel signedness-specific multiply cones, reducing LUT/routing pressure enough to improve Tang Nano placement and Fmax.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (regression, -1.56%): On this Tang Nano RV32M datapath, replacing the shared signed multiplier with an unsigned product plus serial sign-correction subtractors regresses despite narrower inputs, so high-half correction logic and its control/routing can outweigh a one-bit multiplier-width reduction.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r4s2 (improvement, +1.14%): Refining stateless BTFNT to take backward BNE/BLT/BLTU but fall through BEQ/BGE/BGEU better matches CoreMark's counted-loop continuations versus inverted exits, avoiding recurring EX recovery bubbles without adding predictor state or a fetch-path table.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -73.49%): Sharing DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU through unsigned magnitude `/` and `%` expressions can sharply worsen Tang Nano timing because the operators remain separate divider cones while magnitude conversion and sign-restoration mux/negate logic lengthen the arithmetic path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 (regression, -3.50%): In CoreMark, backward BGE/BGEU executions can be biased taken yet still make worse static BTFNT candidates than BNE/BLT/BLTU, so a retired-direction profile alone cannot justify broadening fetch-local loop-continuation funct3 predicates.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (regression, -7.25%): On the Tang Nano RV32M datapath, splitting the shared 33-bit multiplier into four DSP-forced 17-by-17 signed partial products regresses because the widened signed partial-product recombination and extra multiplier placement/routing outweigh the benefit of smaller inferred multipliers.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (regression, -3.47%): Splitting EX/MEM and MEM/WB into lockstep execution and RVFI-trace records does not remove the trace state or its routing, so the added parallel interfaces and control can outweigh localized datapath narrowing on Tang Nano.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (regression, -7.66%): On the fetch-critical Tang Nano predictor path, selecting a 32-bit JAL/branch/sequential displacement before a shared PC adder puts a wide target mux ahead of carry propagation, so parallel PC-relative adders followed by target selection can place and time better despite redundant arithmetic.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
