ting Fmax to 127 MHz near the LUT budget.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU implementation with one shared\\n32-cycle restoring divider that stalls the in-order execute path only for an\\nM divide/remainder instruction. Preserve the existing single-cycle path for\\nall other ALU and multiply operations. The rare divide latency should be\\noutside, or negligible within, the timed CoreMark kernel, while removing the\\nwide combinational divider should improve placement headroom and Fmax enough\\nto raise Fmax times CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the synthesised combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU datapaths while retaining the real and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS semantics for non-divide ALU operations.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/divider_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared signed/unsigned iterative restoring divider with request, busy, completion, quotient, and remainder interfaces, including all RV32M divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 corner cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact control metadata identifying the four divide/remainder ALU operations for execute-stage dispatch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Turn EX into a small execute sequencer: launch divider operations once, hold the instruction and redirect state while busy, and emit the completed quotient or remainder exactly once into EX/MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an execute-busy interlock that freezes younger IF/ID and ID/EX work without duplicating retirements or losing redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divider-busy control into the pipeline stall policy while preserving the existing RVFI retirement boundary and nret=1 IO contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Keep exact RV32M division and remainder edge-case coverage against the new functional-unit result path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed DIV/REM dependency and bus-stall tests to verify a single retirement, correct forwarding, and progress through the multi-cycle EX hold.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the synthesised combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU datapaths while retaining the real and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS semantics for non-divide ALU operations.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/divider_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared signed/unsigned iterative restoring divider with request, busy, completion, quotient, and remainder interfaces, including all RV32M divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 corner cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact control metadata identifying the four divide/remainder ALU operations for execute-stage dispatch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Turn EX into a small execute sequencer: launch divider operations once, hold the instruction and redirect state while busy, and emit the completed quotient or remainder exactly once into EX/MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an execute-busy interlock that freezes younger IF/ID and ID/EX work without duplicating retirements or losing redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divider-busy control into the pipeline stall policy while preserving the existing RVFI retirement boundary and nret=1 IO contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Keep exact RV32M division and remainder edge-case coverage against the new functional-unit result path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed DIV/REM dependency and bus-stall tests to verify a single retirement, correct forwarding, and progress through the multi-cycle EX hold.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1}\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-to-EX load forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline inserts a full bubble for every immediate\\nload consumer, even when the zero-wait data-memory response is already\\navailable combinationally during the load's MEM stage. CoreMark has many\\npointer-chasing and state-update sequences where this fixed interlock\\nreduces useful issue bandwidth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline inserts a full bubble for every immediate\\nload consumer, even when the zero-wait data-memory response is already\\navailable combinationally during the load's MEM stage. CoreMark has many\\npointer-chasing and state-update sequences where this fixed interlock\\nreduces useful issue bandwidth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a late MEM-to-EX bypass that forwards a load's fully extracted and\\nsign/zero-extended dmem result directly to the following instruction's EX\\noperand mux. Remove the normal load-use bubble; when io_dmemReady is low,\\nretain the existing whole-pipeline memory hold so no consumer observes an\\nunacknowledged load. This converts adjacent load-consumer pairs from two\\nexecution cycles to one in the common zero-wait case while preserving\\nin-order retirement, RVFI source values, and precise bus-stall behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the combinationally extracted, sign/zero-extended load value as a dedicated forwarding result alongside the existing MEM/WB capture path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish an EX/MEM load from an ALU producer so the youngest matching source selects the late load-data bypass rather than its address result.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the MEM-stage load-data input to the EX operand muxes and use it for immediately dependent ALU, store-data, branch, and JALR operands.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the fixed load-use bubble while retaining dmem-ready holds, redirect recovery, and all other pipeline interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the MEM load-result bypass and load classification through the forwarding network without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add adjacent load-to-ALU, load-to-store, load-to-branch, and random dmem-backpressure tests covering forwarding, stalls, and one retirement per instruction.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the combinationally extracted, sign/zero-extended load value as a dedicated forwarding result alongside the existing MEM/WB capture path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish an EX/MEM load from an ALU producer so the youngest matching source selects the late load-data bypass rather than its address result.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the MEM-stage load-data input to the EX operand muxes and use it for immediately dependent ALU, store-data, branch, and JALR operands.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the fixed load-use bubble while retaining dmem-ready holds, redirect recovery, and all other pipeline interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the MEM load-result bypass and load classification through the forwarding network without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add adjacent load-to-ALU, load-to-store, load-to-branch, and random dmem-backpressure tests covering forwarding, stalls, and one retirement per instruction.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 323.71, \"delta_pct\": 4.42, \"vs_baseline\": 14.46, \"fmax_mhz\": 131.27, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4055214, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10038, \"ff\": 1918, \"seeds\": [129.07, 131.27, 137.63], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:19:55.081660Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +4.42%): Once the MEM-to-EX load bypass is response-qualified and the pipeline holds on dmem backpressure, removing the load-use interlock recovers IPC on CoreMark's adjacent pointer-chasing and state-update load consumers without weakening in-order correctness.\"}\n9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Ordered single-entry store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Random dmem backpressure currently freezes every pipeline stage whenever a\\nstore reaches MEM, even though its address, data, and byte enables are\\nalready known. CoreMark has regular state and array stores, so these waits\\nunnecessarily prevent independent ALU work and fetch from progressing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Random dmem backpressure currently freezes every pipeline stage whenever a\\nstore reaches MEM, even though its address, data, and byte enables are\\nalready known. CoreMark has regular state and array stores, so these waits\\nunnecessarily prevent independent ALU work and fetch from progressing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a one-entry ordered store buffer between MEM and dmem. A normal RAM\\nstore can leave the pipeline once captured in the buffer, while the buffer\\nretries its bus transaction until io_dmemReady accepts it; a following load\\nwaits for the buffer to drain, preserving RV32 memory order without a\\ncomplex merge path. MMIO stores remain synchronous and drain prior buffered\\nwork before issue, preserving UART and timing-marker ordering. This removes\\nfull-pipeline stalls for store-only backpressure while retaining precise\\nload behavior, RVFI retirement order, and the existing external interface.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry valid/ready store queue that retains aligned address, replicated write data, and byte mask until an acknowledged dmem write.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Classify normal RAM versus strongly ordered MMIO stores, enqueue eligible stores without waiting for dmemReady, and keep loads ordered behind an occupied buffer.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace unconditional store dmem holds with load/MMIO ordering stalls and buffer-full backpressure while retaining all existing redirect priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the store buffer onto the unchanged dmem ports and connect occupancy, drain, and acceptance signals to MEM and hazard control.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add any compact pipeline metadata needed to distinguish buffered stores from loads and strongly ordered MMIO accesses.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add random dmem-stalled store streams, store-to-load ordering, partial-byte stores, UART/MMIO marker ordering, and RVFI single-retirement checks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry valid/ready store queue that retains aligned address, replicated write data, and byte mask until an acknowledged dmem write.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Classify normal RAM versus strongly ordered MMIO stores, enqueue eligible stores without waiting for dmemReady, and keep loads ordered behind an occupied buffer.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace unconditional store dmem holds with load/MMIO ordering stalls and buffer-full backpressure while retaining all existing redirect priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the store buffer onto the unchanged dmem ports and connect occupancy, drain, and acceptance signals to MEM and hazard control.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add any compact pipeline metadata needed to distinguish buffered stores from loads and strongly ordered MMIO accesses.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add random dmem-stalled store streams, store-to-load ordering, partial-byte stores, UART/MMIO marker ordering, and RVFI single-retirement checks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"placement_failed\", \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": \"placement_failed\", \"seeds\": [null, null, null], \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3}\n12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage JALR recovery\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"BTFNT already removes the common direct-JAL and backward-loop recovery\\nbubbles, but JALR still waits for EX to resolve its register target. CoreMark\\nreturns from frequently called helpers through JALR, so that fixed extra\\nstage costs IPC. The failed RAS attempt shows this benefit should not be\\nbought with more target-selection logic on the fetch critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"BTFNT already removes the common direct-JAL and backward-loop recovery\\nbubbles, but JALR still waits for EX to resolve its register target. CoreMark\\nreturns from frequently called helpers through JALR, so that fixed extra\\nstage costs IPC. The failed RAS attempt shows this benefit should not be\\nbought with more target-selection logic on the fetch critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Resolve dependency-free, aligned legal JALRs in ID using the existing\\nwrite-first regfile value and redirect fetch at the ID/EX boundary. Record\\nthe resolved target as that instruction's predicted successor so its normal\\nEX pass does not redirect again. If the immediately older EX instruction\\nwrites JALR's rs1, or the target is misaligned, defer to the present EX\\nresolver and trap path. EX redirects retain priority over an ID redirect,\\nensuring a wrong-path JALR is flushed. This removes one recovery cycle for\\nordinary returns without widening the IF prediction datapath or changing\\nretirement order.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize accepted dependency-free JALRs, compute their aligned rs1-plus-immediate target, emit an early redirect, and carry that target as pred_next_pc while retaining EX fallback cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish older EX recovery from an ID JALR redirect: flush IF for either source, but flush ID only for the older EX redirect so the redirecting JALR still advances once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Arbitrate EX-over-ID redirects, gate early JALR recovery on the absence of an immediately older rs1 producer and memory holds, and preserve the single-channel RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the arbitrated earlier redirect with the existing redirect-over-stall priority, emitting a bubble for the speculative fetch slot.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain the existing resolved-versus-predicted comparison so deferred or dependency-bound JALRs recover in EX and early-resolved JALRs cannot redirect twice.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add return-style JALR, ALU-to-JALR dependency fallback, load-to-JALR under dmem backpressure, misaligned-target trap, and simultaneous older-redirect tests with RVFI PC and order checks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize accepted dependency-free JALRs, compute their aligned rs1-plus-immediate target, emit an early redirect, and carry that target as pred_next_pc while retaining EX fallback cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish older EX recovery from an ID JALR redirect: flush IF for either source, but flush ID only for the older EX redirect so the redirecting JALR still advances once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Arbitrate EX-over-ID redirects, gate early JALR recovery on the absence of an immediately older rs1 producer and memory holds, and preserve the single-channel RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the arbitrated earlier redirect with the existing redirect-over-stall priority, emitting a bubble for the speculative fetch slot.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain the existing resolved-versus-predicted comparison so deferred or dependency-bound JALRs recover in EX and early-resolved JALRs cannot redirect twice.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add return-style JALR, ALU-to-JALR dependency fallback, load-to-JALR under dmem backpressure, misaligned-target trap, and simultaneous older-redirect tests with RVFI PC and order checks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 324.04, \"delta_pct\": -10.37, \"vs_baseline\": 14.57, \"fmax_mhz\": 130.96, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4041507, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10369, \"ff\": 1923, \"seeds\": [132.22, 130.96, 126.37], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:48:20.223113Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4}\n15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Radix-four shared divide unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The RV32M ALU currently elaborates four full-width division and remainder\\nexpressions on every cycle, even though the timed CoreMark path is dominated\\nby ordinary arithmetic, loads, stores, and loop control. Those divider cones\\nconsume LUT and routing resources near the Tang Nano's placement limit and\\ncan constrain Fmax despite divide instructions being infrequent.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The RV32M ALU currently elaborates four full-width division and remainder\\nexpressions on every cycle, even though the timed CoreMark path is dominated\\nby ordinary arithmetic, loads, stores, and loop control. Those divider cones\\nconsume LUT and routing resources near the Tang Nano's placement limit and\\ncan constrain Fmax despite divide instructions being infrequent.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU datapaths with one shared\\nradix-four iterative divider that completes two quotient bits per cycle and\\nholds EX only for the requesting instruction. A 16-cycle bounded operation\\nmakes the rare divide cost small while removing the always-active wide\\ndivider from the ordinary execute path. Preserve the existing immediate\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior so the bounded formal proof remains tractable;\\nreal builds use the iterative unit and retain all RV32M corner semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU expressions from the normal ALU result path while retaining the exact ALTOPS equations used by formal.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/divider_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add one signedness-normalized radix-four iterative divider with request, busy, done, quotient, and remainder interfaces plus divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch divide/remainder operations to the divider exactly once, retain their ID/EX payload while busy, and write the completed selected quotient or remainder into EX/MEM once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add execute-unit busy backpressure that freezes younger fetch and decode work while preserving dmem-stall and redirect priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divider request, completion, and busy signals through the existing single-retire pipeline without changing the external IO or RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact helpers or control classification for identifying divide and remainder ALU operations at execute dispatch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed signed and unsigned DIV/REM dependency, divider-busy, redirect, and dmem-backpressure tests that check exact results and one in-order RVFI retirement per instruction.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU expressions from the normal ALU result path while retaining the exact ALTOPS equations used by formal.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/divider_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add one signedness-normalized radix-four iterative divider with request, busy, done, quotient, and remainder interfaces plus divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch divide/remainder operations to the divider exactly once, retain their ID/EX payload while busy, and write the completed selected quotient or remainder into EX/MEM once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add execute-unit busy backpressure that freezes younger fetch and decode work while preserving dmem-stall and redirect priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divider request, completion, and busy signals through the existing single-retire pipeline without changing the external IO or RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact helpers or control classification for identifying divide and remainder ALU operations at execute dispatch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed signed and unsigned DIV/REM dependency, divider-busy, redirect, and dmem-backpressure tests that check exact results and one in-order RVFI retirement per instruction.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 5}\n18:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Narrow execution and trace pipes\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX/MEM and MEM/WB bundles carry every RVFI-only source value,\\nPC field, branch field, and memory-observation field beside the live\\nexecute datapath. Those wide bundled registers and their broad module\\ninterfaces increase routing pressure around the ALU, forwarding muxes, and\\nmemory stage even though most fields do not affect forward progress.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX/MEM and MEM/WB bundles carry every RVFI-only source value,\\nPC field, branch field, and memory-observation field beside the live\\nexecute datapath. Those wide bundled registers and their broad module\\ninterfaces increase routing pressure around the ALU, forwarding muxes, and\\nmemory stage even though most fields do not affect forward progress.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Partition the pipeline into a narrow execution pipe and a lockstep\\nretirement-trace pipe. The execution pipe keeps only data and control that\\nthe following stage consumes for architectural execution, while a sideband\\ntrace record carries RVFI-only operand, PC, instruction, and memory\\nobservations to the same single retirement boundary. Both pipes use the\\nidentical valid/hold/flush enables, so retirement order and every RVFI\\nvalue remain unchanged. Reducing wide high-fanout execute-stage bundles\\nshould improve Tang Nano placement and Fmax without adding an IPC penalty.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Split the existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB payload definitions into compact execution records and lockstep RVFI trace records, keeping all architectural fields represented exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Register only live ALU, store-data, destination, control, and valid state on the execution path while emitting a separately registered trace record containing post-forward source observations and resolved PC metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrow execution record for the memory transaction, update the trace record with aligned-memory masks and raw read/write observations, and retain identical hold behavior for both records.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select writeback data from the compact retirement execution record without depending on RVFI-only trace fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the paired records through forwarding and retirement, constructing channel-0 RVFI exclusively from the trace record plus the unchanged writeback result.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add equivalence-oriented pipeline cases covering ALU, load, store, branch, redirect, and random bus stalls, checking unchanged RVFI operands, memory fields, order, and single retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Split the existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB payload definitions into compact execution records and lockstep RVFI trace records, keeping all architectural fields represented exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Register only live ALU, store-data, destination, control, and valid state on the execution path while emitting a separately registered trace record containing post-forward source observations and resolved PC metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrow execution record for the memory transaction, update the trace record with aligned-memory masks and raw read/write observations, and retain identical hold behavior for both records.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select writeback data from the compact retirement execution record without depending on RVFI-only trace fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the paired records through forwarding and retirement, constructing channel-0 RVFI exclusively from the trace record plus the unchanged writeback result.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add equivalence-oriented pipeline cases covering ALU, load, store, branch, redirect, and random bus stalls, checking unchanged RVFI operands, memory fields, order, and single retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 352.96, \"delta_pct\": -3.47, \"vs_baseline\": 24.8, \"fmax_mhz\": 142.71, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4043217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10324, \"ff\": 1922, \"seeds\": [148.3, 141.7, 142.71], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T02:16:23.649583Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (regression, -3.47%): Splitting EX/MEM and MEM/WB into lockstep execution and RVFI-trace records does not remove the trace state or its routing, so the added parallel interfaces and control can outweigh localized datapath narrowing on Tang Nano.\"}\n21:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Resetless valid-tag register file\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The integer register file resets all 1,024 data bits synchronously even\\nthough a post-reset architectural read only needs to observe zero until\\nthat register is first written. This large resettable flop bank and its\\nasynchronous read muxes add local routing pressure beside decode.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The integer register file resets all 1,024 data bits synchronously even\\nthough a post-reset architectural read only needs to observe zero until\\nthat register is first written. This large resettable flop bank and its\\nasynchronous read muxes add local routing pressure beside decode.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the resettable register data array with a resetless, distributed-RAM\\nfriendly array plus a 32-bit resettable valid mask. A read returns zero for\\nx0 or an invalid entry, while a write marks its destination valid; the\\nexisting same-cycle write-first bypass remains highest priority. Thus reset\\nstill presents every architectural register as zero without resetting the\\n1,024 stored data bits. Removing reset routing from the wide array and\\nallowing LUT-RAM inference should improve packing and Fmax without changing\\npipeline latency, forwarding, RVFI values, or CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement an unreset data array with distributed-RAM inference guidance, a resettable per-register validity mask, zero-on-invalid reads, and preserved x0/write-first behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Add reset, stale-data-after-reset, x0, write-first, and independent dual-read checks for the valid-tag register-file implementation.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement an unreset data array with distributed-RAM inference guidance, a resettable per-register validity mask, zero-on-invalid reads, and preserved x0/write-first behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Add reset, stale-data-after-reset, x0, write-first, and independent dual-read checks for the valid-tag register-file implementation.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 317.99, \"delta_pct\": -13.03, \"vs_baseline\": 12.44, \"fmax_mhz\": 128.57, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4043217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 7817, \"ff\": 1122, \"seeds\": [128.57, 144.28, 107.61], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T02:28:34.695951Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 (regression, -13.03%): For a small asynchronous-read register file, replacing data reset with a valid mask adds per-read valid gating and mask fanout on the decode-local read path, which can cost more timing and routing than eliminating reset on the data flops saves.\"}\n24:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic instruction fetch queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current frontend stops its PC and discards each fetch opportunity whenever\\na data-memory transaction stalls the in-order pipeline.  The independent\\ninstruction and data buses in the benchmark permit useful instruction fetch\\nduring those holds, creating a reserve that can absorb later random imem\\nbackpressure without changing architectural issue order.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current frontend stops its PC and discards each fetch opportunity whenever\\na data-memory transaction stalls the in-order pipeline.  The independent\\ninstruction and data buses in the benchmark permit useful instruction fetch\\nduring those holds, creating a reserve that can absorb later random imem\\nbackpressure without changing architectural issue order.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF-to-ID handoff with a compact two-entry elastic\\nfetch queue.  Fetch continues into an empty queue slot while MEM is held for\\ndmem backpressure, and ID consumes queued entries only when it can advance.\\nEach entry carries PC, instruction, and the existing predicted successor;\\nan EX redirect flushes all speculative entries and restarts fetch at the\\nresolved target.  This preserves the proven static prediction policy and\\nsingle-retire pipeline while overlapping independent imem service with data\\nstalls, improving CoreMark IPC in the random-stall cosimulation.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a two-entry valid/ready fetch queue that records PC, instruction, and predicted successor, permits fetch during downstream holds, and flushes precisely on EX redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the direct IF/ID handoff wiring with fetch enqueue/dequeue control and preserve redirect-over-stall priority at the frontend boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate frontend queue consumption from dmem pipeline holding so a full or flushed queue backpressures fetch without allowing younger instructions into held execute state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume only a valid queue head when ID/EX can advance, retaining bubble, flush, and RVFI source-observation semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and random imem/dmem-stall tests for queue fill and drain, redirect flushing, branch prediction recovery, RVFI order, and no duplicate retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a two-entry valid/ready fetch queue that records PC, instruction, and predicted successor, permits fetch during downstream holds, and flushes precisely on EX redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the direct IF/ID handoff wiring with fetch enqueue/dequeue control and preserve redirect-over-stall priority at the frontend boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate frontend queue consumption from dmem pipeline holding so a full or flushed queue backpressures fetch without allowing younger instructions into held execute state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume only a valid queue head when ID/EX can advance, retaining bubble, flush, and RVFI source-observation semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and random imem/dmem-stall tests for queue fill and drain, redirect flushing, branch prediction recovery, RVFI order, and no duplicate retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: coremark.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 8}\n27:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Decoupled two-entry prefetch buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Random instruction and data bus stalls currently stop the shared frontend,\\neven when the independent instruction port could have accepted useful work.\\nCoreMark's in-order backend needs only one instruction per cycle, so a small\\nbuffer can turn independent service opportunities into tolerance for later\\ninstruction-memory backpressure without widening the predictor datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Random instruction and data bus stalls currently stop the shared frontend,\\neven when the independent instruction port could have accepted useful work.\\nCoreMark's in-order backend needs only one instruction per cycle, so a small\\nbuffer can turn independent service opportunities into tolerance for later\\ninstruction-memory backpressure without widening the predictor datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF-to-ID handoff with a two-entry, explicitly\\nenqueue/dequeue-controlled prefetch buffer.  An imem word is enqueued only\\non io_imemReady, and fetch may fill a vacant slot while ID/EX is held by a\\ndmem stall; ID dequeues only when its register advances.  Each entry carries\\nits PC, instruction, and already-computed predicted successor, keeping the\\nexisting static predictor off the queue-control critical path.  An EX\\nredirect discards every unconsumed speculative entry and restarts fetch from\\nthe resolved PC, while the older redirecting instruction proceeds normally.\\nThis is a narrow frontend elasticity change that preserves in-order issue,\\nRVFI retirement, and the proven branch policy while covering a portion of\\nthe independently injected imem stalls.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement an accepted-request two-entry prefetch buffer whose entries retain PC, instruction, and the existing predicted next PC; flush unconsumed entries atomically on EX redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire frontend enqueue/dequeue availability separately from backend advance, retaining redirect-over-stall PC priority and the unchanged external imem interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace direct IF/ID stall assumptions with queue backpressure and dequeue gating, while preserving whole-pipeline dmem holds and redirect flush priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch only a valid queue-head entry when decode is permitted to advance, preserving reset, flush, operand-read, and RVFI-source semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized imem/dmem-stall coverage for queue fill and drain, accepted-request accounting, redirect flushes, prediction recovery, and strict single-retirement RVFI order.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement an accepted-request two-entry prefetch buffer whose entries retain PC, instruction, and the existing predicted next PC; flush unconsumed entries atomically on EX redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire frontend enqueue/dequeue availability separately from backend advance, retaining redirect-over-stall PC priority and the unchanged external imem interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace direct IF/ID stall assumptions with queue backpressure and dequeue gating, while preserving whole-pipeline dmem holds and redirect flush priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch only a valid queue-head entry when decode is permitted to advance, preserving reset, flush, operand-read, and RVFI-source semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized imem/dmem-stall coverage for queue fill and drain, accepted-request accounting, redirect flushes, prediction recovery, and strict single-retirement RVFI order.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 150.33, \"delta_pct\": -61.53, \"vs_baseline\": -46.85, \"fmax_mhz\": 60.53, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4026396, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10425, \"ff\": 2110, \"seeds\": [60.53, 63.71, 58.72], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T02:57:24.036037Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 9}\n30:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Tiny direct-mapped instruction cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The performance cosimulation injects independent instruction-bus stalls on\\nevery fetched word, while CoreMark repeatedly executes a compact set of hot\\nloop bodies. The current combinational fetch path must wait for each such\\nbus acceptance, and prior queue designs added enough wide control and data\\nrouting to lose far more Fmax than their overlap recovered.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The performance cosimulation injects independent instruction-bus stalls on\\nevery fetched word, while CoreMark repeatedly executes a compact set of hot\\nloop bodies. The current combinational fetch path must wait for each such\\nbus acceptance, and prior queue designs added enough wide control and data\\nrouting to lose far more Fmax than their overlap recovered.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small 16-line, one-word-per-line direct-mapped instruction cache\\nbetween IF and the existing instruction-memory port. Each cache line holds\\na valid bit, word address tag, and instruction; a hit supplies the current PC's word\\nwithout requiring io_imemReady, while a miss retains the existing request\\nand fill-on-accepted-response behavior. Keep the present static JAL and\\nbackward-loop prediction unchanged and invalidate entries only on reset,\\nnot on redirects. This is a\\nbounded structural source of stall tolerance that avoids a FIFO's\\nenqueue/dequeue paths and should capture CoreMark's repeatedly fetched hot\\ninstructions after warm-up.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/icache.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact 16-line direct-mapped instruction cache with one word per line plus tag, valid, accepted-fill, hit, and miss-request interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use cache hits as valid fetch responses, hold a miss PC until its bus response is accepted, and preserve the existing fetch-local prediction and redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire the instruction cache between the unchanged imem ports and IF, exposing cache miss state to pipeline control without altering the core IO or RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace raw imem-ready flushing with a qualified cache-miss wait so cache hits continue to issue during external instruction-bus stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add cache cold-miss, hit-under-imem-stall, refill, redirect, and mixed dmem-stall tests checking in-order RVFI PCs, orders, and no duplicate fetch retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/icache.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact 16-line direct-mapped instruction cache with one word per line plus tag, valid, accepted-fill, hit, and miss-request interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use cache hits as valid fetch responses, hold a miss PC until its bus response is accepted, and preserve the existing fetch-local prediction and redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire the instruction cache between the unchanged imem ports and IF, exposing cache miss state to pipeline control without altering the core IO or RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace raw imem-ready flushing with a qualified cache-miss wait so cache hits continue to issue during external instruction-bus stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add cache cold-miss, hit-under-imem-stall, refill, redirect, and mixed dmem-stall tests checking in-order RVFI PCs, orders, and no duplicate fetch retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 293.47, \"delta_pct\": -43.09, \"vs_baseline\": 3.77, \"fmax_mhz\": 144.63, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4928248, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10798, \"ff\": 2042, \"seeds\": [144.63, 138.2, 149.1], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:18:23.384707Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (regression, -43.09%): A one-word direct-mapped IF cache can lose badly when its tag compare, hit-data mux, and miss-wait/valid gating sit on the fetch-critical path (and each fill is bubbled), outweighing CoreMark hot-loop locality and avoided instruction-bus stalls.\"}\n33:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-stage completion and retirement\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The existing MEM/WB record transports a full RVFI snapshot through an\\nadditional pipeline register after all architectural memory work has\\ncompleted. This wide record and its writeback/RVFI fanout consume routing\\nnear a design already above the LUT target, without improving steady-state\\nissue bandwidth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The existing MEM/WB record transports a full RVFI snapshot through an\\nadditional pipeline register after all architectural memory work has\\ncompleted. This wide record and its writeback/RVFI fanout consume routing\\nnear a design already above the LUT target, without improving steady-state\\nissue bandwidth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Collapse the MEM/WB stage into an acknowledged MEM completion boundary.\\nRetire and write the register file directly from the EX/MEM instruction\\nonce a load or store is accepted (or immediately for a non-memory op), and\\nconstruct RVFI from that same completion snapshot. Keep only a narrow\\nregistered writeback-bypass record for the one older producer needed by\\nEX forwarding. This removes the wide MEM/WB state and its broad routing\\nwhile keeping the zero-bubble load bypass, in-order retirement, and the\\nexisting stall behavior; it should improve placement/Fmax without adding\\nfetch-path logic or an IPC penalty.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the full MEM/WB payload with a compact writeback-bypass record and define the MEM completion fields needed for direct RVFI construction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose an accepted completion snapshot, including qualified load data, aligned memory observations, trap qualification, and a retire-valid signal that is suppressed while an active memory request is unacknowledged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select direct MEM-completion writeback data and maintain only the narrow registered producer result required for the older EX forwarding case.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retarget the older-producer forwarding input from the removed MEM/WB record to the compact writeback-bypass record, retaining youngest-producer priority and the load bypass.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the revised compact older-writeback forwarding source without changing EX/MEM execution, branch resolution, or same-cycle load forwarding.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify memory completion and pipeline holds so dmem backpressure cannot cause a direct-MEM retirement or register write to repeat.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Move channel-0 RVFI, rvfi_order advancement, and regfile write enable to the MEM completion boundary while preserving the nret=1 top-level port contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add ALU/load/store completion-boundary tests under random imem/dmem stalls, checking exact RVFI fields, strict order, writeback forwarding, and one retirement per accepted instruction.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the full MEM/WB payload with a compact writeback-bypass record and define the MEM completion fields needed for direct RVFI construction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose an accepted completion snapshot, including qualified load data, aligned memory observations, trap qualification, and a retire-valid signal that is suppressed while an active memory request is unacknowledged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select direct MEM-completion writeback data and maintain only the narrow registered producer result required for the older EX forwarding case.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retarget the older-producer forwarding input from the removed MEM/WB record to the compact writeback-bypass record, retaining youngest-producer priority and the load bypass.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the revised compact older-writeback forwarding source without changing EX/MEM execution, branch resolution, or same-cycle load forwarding.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify memory completion and pipeline holds so dmem backpressure cannot cause a direct-MEM retirement or register write to repeat.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Move channel-0 RVFI, rvfi_order advancement, and regfile write enable to the MEM completion boundary while preserving the nret=1 top-level port contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add ALU/load/store completion-boundary tests under random imem/dmem stalls, checking exact RVFI fields, strict order, writeback forwarding, and one retirement per accepted instruction.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 11}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && printf '\\\\n--- structural history ---\\\\n' && jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"structural\\\") | [.id, .title, (.outcome // \\\"pending\\\"), (.fitness // \\\"-\\\"), (.delta_pct // \\\"-\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && printf '\\\\n--- schema ---\\\\n' && sed -n '1,220p' schemas/hypothesis.schema.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && printf '\\\\n--- structural history ---\\\\n' && jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"structural\\\") | [.id, .title, (.outcome // \\\"pending\\\"), (.fitness // \\\"-\\\"), (.delta_pct // \\\"-\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && printf '\\\\n--- schema ---\\\\n' && sed -n '1,220p' schemas/hypothesis.schema.json\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (improvement, +9.61%): Carrying IF's predicted next PC to EX lets a static BTFNT policy avoid recovery bubbles on CoreMark's frequent backward counted-loop branches (and direct JALs), making low-cost fetch-side control prediction an effective IPC optimization without a BTB.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): A zero-bubble MEM-to-EX load bypass must be qualified by the actual memory-response handshake (and freeze the consumer until then), since selecting combinational load data before acceptance corrupts dependent register results under dmem backpressure.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +4.42%): Once the MEM-to-EX load bypass is response-qualified and the pipeline holds on dmem backpressure, removing the load-use interlock recovers IPC on CoreMark's adjacent pointer-chasing and state-update load consumers without weakening in-order correctness.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, -8.15%): A RAS-top mux and canonical-return decode on the IF next-PC path can cost more timing than CoreMark gains from avoiding return recoveries, so return prediction must preserve the fetch critical path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (improvement, +11.68%): Feeding opcode-selected sign/zero-extended 33-bit operands into one signed multiplier preserves all RV32M high-half products while eliminating parallel signedness-specific multiply cones, reducing LUT/routing pressure enough to improve Tang Nano placement and Fmax.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (regression, -1.56%): On this Tang Nano RV32M datapath, replacing the shared signed multiplier with an unsigned product plus serial sign-correction subtractors regresses despite narrower inputs, so high-half correction logic and its control/routing can outweigh a one-bit multiplier-width reduction.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r4s2 (improvement, +1.14%): Refining stateless BTFNT to take backward BNE/BLT/BLTU but fall through BEQ/BGE/BGEU better matches CoreMark's counted-loop continuations versus inverted exits, avoiding recurring EX recovery bubbles without adding predictor state or a fetch-path table.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -73.49%): Sharing DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU through unsigned magnitude `/` and `%` expressions can sharply worsen Tang Nano timing because the operators remain separate divider cones while magnitude conversion and sign-restoration mux/negate logic lengthen the arithmetic path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 (regression, -3.50%): In CoreMark, backward BGE/BGEU executions can be biased taken yet still make worse static BTFNT candidates than BNE/BLT/BLTU, so a retired-direction profile alone cannot justify broadening fetch-local loop-continuation funct3 predicates.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (regression, -7.25%): On the Tang Nano RV32M datapath, splitting the shared 33-bit multiplier into four DSP-forced 17-by-17 signed partial products regresses because the widened signed partial-product recombination and extra multiplier placement/routing outweigh the benefit of smaller inferred multipliers.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (regression, -3.47%): Splitting EX/MEM and MEM/WB into lockstep execution and RVFI-trace records does not remove the trace state or its routing, so the added parallel interfaces and control can outweigh localized datapath narrowing on Tang Nano.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (regression, -7.66%): On the fetch-critical Tang Nano predictor path, selecting a 32-bit JAL/branch/sequential displacement before a shared PC adder puts a wide target mux ahead of carry propagation, so parallel PC-relative adders followed by target selection can place and time better despite redundant arithmetic.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (regression, -12.63%): Explicitly sharing 32-bit branch comparisons can worsen the timing-critical EX redirect cone when the shared relation nets and predicate mux add fanout and selection delay that direct per-funct3 relational expressions let synthesis optimize locally.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 (regression, -13.03%): For a small asynchronous-read register file, replacing data reset with a valid mask adds per-read valid gating and mask fanout on the decode-local read path, which can cost more timing and routing than eliminating reset on the data flops saves.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r7s2 (regression, -22.17%): On the fetch-critical Tang Nano next-PC cone, hand-minimizing the BNE/BLT/BLTU funct3 predicate can map and place worse than direct equality decodes despite identical branch behavior, so Boolean gate-count reduction alone is not a reliable timing optimization.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (improvement, +6.87%): Partitioning registered ALU control into an RV32I/RV32M class bit plus class-local opcode keeps CoreMark's ordinary arithmetic out of the combined base-plus-M result-selection mux, shortening the control-to-EX cone without changing execution semantics.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r8s2 (regression, -21.43%): Resolving source-ready conditional branches in ID puts two-source comparison, EX/MEM producer-scoreboarding, PC-target generation, and the ID-to-IF redirect mux on the front-end critical path, costing more timing than CoreMark recovers from a one-cycle-earlier misprediction fix.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r9s0 (improvement, +31.97%): Further partitioning RV32I ALU control into a coarse result class and class-local sub-operation keeps CoreMark's dominant ADD/SUB and AUIPC address-generation path out of logic, compare, shift, and LUI selection cones, substantially shortening the registered-control-to-EX routing/mux path on Tang Nano without changing latency.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 (regression, -11.98%): Moving a one-bit direction table to ID avoids fetch lookup but its PC-indexed hint, predicted-successor override, and EX/ID redirect arbitration still cost more than CoreMark gains from correcting the remaining forward/range branches after static BNE/BLT/BLTU loop prediction.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (regression, -43.09%): A one-word direct-mapped IF cache can lose badly when its tag compare, hit-data mux, and miss-wait/valid gating sit on the fetch-critical path (and each fill is bubbled), outweighing CoreMark hot-loop locality and avoided instruction-bus stalls.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (regression, -7.49%): CoreMark's backward BLT/BLTU branches remain valuable static loop-continuation predictions alongside BNE, so making them fall through reintroduces more EX recovery bubbles than it avoids from mispredicted range predicates.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r11s0 (regression, -6.47%): Replacing encoded forwarding selects with per-source valid channels does not inherently shorten EX timing: a shared load/ALU producer mux plus priority-valid routing can add fanout and control delay ahead of both CoreMark-critical ADD/address and branch-compare operands.\n\n--- structural history ---\nhyp-20260710-002-r1s1\tIterative divider execution unit\tbroken\t-\t-\nhyp-20260710-002-r2s1\tMEM-to-EX load forwarding\timprovement\t323.71\t4.42\nhyp-20260710-002-r3s1\tOrdered single-entry store buffer\tplacement_failed\t-\t-\nhyp-20260710-002-r4s1\tDecode-stage JALR recovery\tregression\t324.04\t-10.37\nhyp-20260710-002-r5s1\tRadix-four shared divide unit\tbroken\t-\t-\nhyp-20260710-002-r6s1\tNarrow execution and trace pipes\tregression\t352.96\t-3.47\nhyp-20260710-002-r7s1\tResetless valid-tag register file\tregression\t317.99\t-13.03\nhyp-20260710-002-r8s1\tElastic instruction fetch queue\tbroken\t-\t-\nhyp-20260710-002-r9s1\tDecoupled two-entry prefetch buffer\tregression\t150.33\t-61.53\nhyp-20260711-002-r10s1\tTiny direct-mapped instruction cache\tregression\t293.47\t-43.09\nhyp-20260711-002-r11s1\tMEM-stage completion and retirement\tbroken\t-\t-\n\n--- schema ---\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"id\",\"title\",\"category\",\"motivation\",\"hypothesis\",\"expected_impact\",\"changes\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"id\":       {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^hyp-[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{3}(-r[0-9]+s[0-9]+)?$\"},\n    \"title\":    {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"file\":        {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"Must be under rtl/ (any extension) or be a test/test_*.py cocotb suite. Anything else is rejected by the runtime sandbox check.\",\n            \"pattern\": \"^(rtl/[^.].*|test/test_[^/]+\\\\.py)$\"\n          },\n          \"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The history rules out wide fetch-state additions, early JALR work, and broad MEM/WB restructuring. I’m checking the remaining structural candidates against the current timing/IPC measurements so the proposal targets a different bottleneck."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"structural\\\") | \\\"\\\\(.id) | \\\\(.title) | \\\\(.outcome // \\\"pending\\\") | fmax=\\\\(.fmax_mhz // \\\"-\\\") cycles=\\\\(.cycles // \\\"-\\\") lut=\\\\(.lut4 // \\\"-\\\")\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis // \\\"\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && printf '\\\\n--- latest overall ---\\\\n' && tail -n 8 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | jq -r '[.id,.category,.outcome,(.fitness // \\\"-\\\"),(.fmax_mhz // \\\"-\\\"),(.cycles // \\\"-\\\"),(.lut4 // \\\"-\\\")]|@tsv' && printf '\\\\n--- RTL file sizes ---\\\\n' && wc -l cores/bench/rtl/*.sv\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"structural\\\") | \\\"\\\\(.id) | \\\\(.title) | \\\\(.outcome // \\\"pending\\\") | fmax=\\\\(.fmax_mhz // \\\"-\\\") cycles=\\\\(.cycles // \\\"-\\\") lut=\\\\(.lut4 // \\\"-\\\")\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis // \\\"\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && printf '\\\\n--- latest overall ---\\\\n' && tail -n 8 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | jq -r '[.id,.category,.outcome,(.fitness // \\\"-\\\"),(.fmax_mhz // \\\"-\\\"),(.cycles // \\\"-\\\"),(.lut4 // \\\"-\\\")]|@tsv' && printf '\\\\n--- RTL file sizes ---\\\\n' && wc -l cores/bench/rtl/*.sv\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260710-002-r1s1 | Iterative divider execution unit | broken | fmax=- cycles=- lut=-\nReplace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU implementation with one shared\n32-cycle restoring divider that stalls the in-order execute path only for an\nM divide/remainder instruction. Preserve the existing single-cycle path for\nall other ALU and multiply operations. The rare divide latency should be\noutside, or negligible within, the timed CoreMark kernel, while removing the\nwide combinational divider should improve placement headroom and Fmax enough\nto raise Fmax times CoreMark IPC.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-002-r2s1 | MEM-to-EX load forwarding | improvement | fmax=131.27 cycles=4055214 lut=10038\nAdd a late MEM-to-EX bypass that forwards a load's fully extracted and\nsign/zero-extended dmem result directly to the following instruction's EX\noperand mux. Remove the normal load-use bubble; when io_dmemReady is low,\nretain the existing whole-pipeline memory hold so no consumer observes an\nunacknowledged load. This converts adjacent load-consumer pairs from two\nexecution cycles to one in the common zero-wait case while preserving\nin-order retirement, RVFI source values, and precise bus-stall behavior.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-002-r3s1 | Ordered single-entry store buffer | placement_failed | fmax=- cycles=- lut=-\nInsert a one-entry ordered store buffer between MEM and dmem. A normal RAM\nstore can leave the pipeline once captured in the buffer, while the buffer\nretries its bus transaction until io_dmemReady accepts it; a following load\nwaits for the buffer to drain, preserving RV32 memory order without a\ncomplex merge path. MMIO stores remain synchronous and drain prior buffered\nwork before issue, preserving UART and timing-marker ordering. This removes\nfull-pipeline stalls for store-only backpressure while retaining precise\nload behavior, RVFI retirement order, and the existing external interface.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-002-r4s1 | Decode-stage JALR recovery | regression | fmax=130.96 cycles=4041507 lut=10369\nResolve dependency-free, aligned legal JALRs in ID using the existing\nwrite-first regfile value and redirect fetch at the ID/EX boundary. Record\nthe resolved target as that instruction's predicted successor so its normal\nEX pass does not redirect again. If the immediately older EX instruction\nwrites JALR's rs1, or the target is misaligned, defer to the present EX\nresolver and trap path. EX redirects retain priority over an ID redirect,\nensuring a wrong-path JALR is flushed. This removes one recovery cycle for\nordinary returns without widening the IF prediction datapath or changing\nretirement order.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-002-r5s1 | Radix-four shared divide unit | broken | fmax=- cycles=- lut=-\nReplace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU datapaths with one shared\nradix-four iterative divider that completes two quotient bits per cycle and\nholds EX only for the requesting instruction. A 16-cycle bounded operation\nmakes the rare divide cost small while removing the always-active wide\ndivider from the ordinary execute path. Preserve the existing immediate\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior so the bounded formal proof remains tractable;\nreal builds use the iterative unit and retain all RV32M corner semantics.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-002-r6s1 | Narrow execution and trace pipes | regression | fmax=142.71 cycles=4043217 lut=10324\nPartition the pipeline into a narrow execution pipe and a lockstep\nretirement-trace pipe. The execution pipe keeps only data and control that\nthe following stage consumes for architectural execution, while a sideband\ntrace record carries RVFI-only operand, PC, instruction, and memory\nobservations to the same single retirement boundary. Both pipes use the\nidentical valid/hold/flush enables, so retirement order and every RVFI\nvalue remain unchanged. Reducing wide high-fanout execute-stage bundles\nshould improve Tang Nano placement and Fmax without adding an IPC penalty.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-002-r7s1 | Resetless valid-tag register file | regression | fmax=128.57 cycles=4043217 lut=7817\nReplace the resettable register data array with a resetless, distributed-RAM\nfriendly array plus a 32-bit resettable valid mask. A read returns zero for\nx0 or an invalid entry, while a write marks its destination valid; the\nexisting same-cycle write-first bypass remains highest priority. Thus reset\nstill presents every architectural register as zero without resetting the\n1,024 stored data bits. Removing reset routing from the wide array and\nallowing LUT-RAM inference should improve packing and Fmax without changing\npipeline latency, forwarding, RVFI values, or CoreMark IPC.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-002-r8s1 | Elastic instruction fetch queue | broken | fmax=- cycles=- lut=-\nReplace the combinational IF-to-ID handoff with a compact two-entry elastic\nfetch queue.  Fetch continues into an empty queue slot while MEM is held for\ndmem backpressure, and ID consumes queued entries only when it can advance.\nEach entry carries PC, instruction, and the existing predicted successor;\nan EX redirect flushes all speculative entries and restarts fetch at the\nresolved target.  This preserves the proven static prediction policy and\nsingle-retire pipeline while overlapping independent imem service with data\nstalls, improving CoreMark IPC in the random-stall cosimulation.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-002-r9s1 | Decoupled two-entry prefetch buffer | regression | fmax=60.53 cycles=4026396 lut=10425\nReplace the combinational IF-to-ID handoff with a two-entry, explicitly\nenqueue/dequeue-controlled prefetch buffer.  An imem word is enqueued only\non io_imemReady, and fetch may fill a vacant slot while ID/EX is held by a\ndmem stall; ID dequeues only when its register advances.  Each entry carries\nits PC, instruction, and already-computed predicted successor, keeping the\nexisting static predictor off the queue-control critical path.  An EX\nredirect discards every unconsumed speculative entry and restarts fetch from\nthe resolved PC, while the older redirecting instruction proceeds normally.\nThis is a narrow frontend elasticity change that preserves in-order issue,\nRVFI retirement, and the proven branch policy while covering a portion of\nthe independently injected imem stalls.\n\n\nhyp-20260711-002-r10s1 | Tiny direct-mapped instruction cache | regression | fmax=144.63 cycles=4928248 lut=10798\nAdd a small 16-line, one-word-per-line direct-mapped instruction cache\nbetween IF and the existing instruction-memory port. Each cache line holds\na valid bit, word address tag, and instruction; a hit supplies the current PC's word\nwithout requiring io_imemReady, while a miss retains the existing request\nand fill-on-accepted-response behavior. Keep the present static JAL and\nbackward-loop prediction unchanged and invalidate entries only on reset,\nnot on redirects. This is a\nbounded structural source of stall tolerance that avoids a FIFO's\nenqueue/dequeue paths and should capture CoreMark's repeatedly fetched hot\ninstructions after warm-up.\n\n\nhyp-20260711-002-r11s1 | MEM-stage completion and retirement | broken | fmax=- cycles=- lut=-\nCollapse the MEM/WB stage into an acknowledged MEM completion boundary.\nRetire and write the register file directly from the EX/MEM instruction\nonce a load or store is accepted (or immediately for a non-memory op), and\nconstruct RVFI from that same completion snapshot. Keep only a narrow\nregistered writeback-bypass record for the one older producer needed by\nEX forwarding. This removes the wide MEM/WB state and its broad routing\nwhile keeping the zero-bubble load bypass, in-order retirement, and the\nexisting stall behavior; it should improve placement/Fmax without adding\nfetch-path logic or an IPC penalty.\n\n\n\n--- latest overall ---\nhyp-20260710-002-r9s1\tstructural\tregression\t150.33\t60.53\t4026396\t10425\nhyp-20260710-003-r9s2\tpredictor\tregression\t343.97\t138.41\t4023945\t10098\nhyp-20260711-001-r10s0\tmicro_opt\tregression\t397.38\t160.67\t4043217\t10490\nhyp-20260711-002-r10s1\tstructural\tregression\t293.47\t144.63\t4928248\t10798\nhyp-20260711-003-r10s2\tpredictor\tregression\t477.09\t195.54\t4098610\t10509\nhyp-20260711-001-r11s0\tmicro_opt\tregression\t482.31\t195.01\t4043217\t10177\nhyp-20260711-002-r11s1\tstructural\tbroken\t-\t-\t-\t-\nhyp-20260711-003-r11s2\tpredictor\tregression\t342.87\t139.61\t4071751\t10249\n\n--- RTL file sizes ---\n       4 cores/bench/rtl/00_core_pkg.sv\n     153 cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n     243 cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n     157 cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n     251 cores/bench/rtl/decoder.sv\n     177 cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n      42 cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n      72 cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n     121 cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n      93 cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n      42 cores/bench/rtl/imm_gen.sv\n     167 cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n      58 cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\n      27 cores/bench/rtl/wb_stage.sv\n    1607 total\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.id == \\\"hyp-20260710-001-r9s0\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r11s0\\\") | {id,title,outcome,fitness,delta_pct,fmax_mhz,cycles,lut4,ff,implementation_notes}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && rg -n 'RVFI|rvfi|critical|timing|Fmax|CoreMark|load|store|branch' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -n 25\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.id == \\\"hyp-20260710-001-r9s0\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r11s0\\\") | {id,title,outcome,fitness,delta_pct,fmax_mhz,cycles,lut4,ff,implementation_notes}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && rg -n 'RVFI|rvfi|critical|timing|Fmax|CoreMark|load|store|branch' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -n 25\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r9s0\",\n  \"title\": \"Fine-grained base ALU partition\",\n  \"outcome\": \"improvement\",\n  \"fitness\": 515.7,\n  \"delta_pct\": 31.97,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 208.51,\n  \"cycles\": 4043217,\n  \"lut4\": 10525,\n  \"ff\": 1926,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r11s0\",\n  \"title\": \"Compact EX forwarding topology\",\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 482.31,\n  \"delta_pct\": -6.47,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 195.01,\n  \"cycles\": 4043217,\n  \"lut4\": 10177,\n  \"ff\": 1926,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\"\n}\n10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny BTFNT-seeded branch direction table\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Static BTFNT already removes the common loop-back recovery bubble, but it\\ncannot learn the bias of forward conditional branches or branches whose\\ndirection differs from their displacement. CoreMark executes such tests in\\nits state machines and list-processing paths. The failed RAS shows that a\\npredictor must not add a wide target mux or substantial decode to fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Static BTFNT already removes the common loop-back recovery bubble, but it\\ncannot learn the bias of forward conditional branches or branches whose\\ndirection differs from their displacement. CoreMark executes such tests in\\nits state machines and list-processing paths. The failed RAS shows that a\\npredictor must not add a wide target mux or substantial decode to fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small direct-mapped, two-bit conditional-branch direction table that\\noverrides BTFNT only after that PC has been observed. On a cold entry, IF\\nkeeps the existing backward-taken/forward-not-taken decision and continues\\nto derive the target solely from the instruction immediate. EX trains the\\ntable with the resolved conditional-branch outcome exactly once when the\\ninstruction advances. This learns stable forward and atypical branch bias\\nwhile preserving the existing narrow fetch target path, direct-JAL policy,\\nrecovery mechanism, and precise behavior under memory stalls.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped valid-plus-two-bit direction table with combinational PC-indexed lookup, BTFNT-compatible cold entries, and one qualified EX-stage training port.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the predictor direction only for validated conditional branches, retaining immediate-only target formation, direct-JAL prediction, and BTFNT fallback on cold table entries.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the resolved conditional-branch PC and architectural direction as a training event only when EX advances, excluding stalled or trapped branch targets.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire the direction predictor between IF and EX without changing the nret=1 top-level or RVFI retirement interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed cold-start, saturating-counter, forward-biased, loop-exit, misprediction-recovery, and dmem-stall training tests with RVFI PC-order checks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped valid-plus-two-bit direction table with combinational PC-indexed lookup, BTFNT-compatible cold entries, and one qualified EX-stage training port.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the predictor direction only for validated conditional branches, retaining immediate-only target formation, direct-JAL prediction, and BTFNT fallback on cold table entries.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the resolved conditional-branch PC and architectural direction as a training event only when EX advances, excluding stalled or trapped branch targets.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire the direction predictor between IF and EX without changing the nret=1 top-level or RVFI retirement interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed cold-start, saturating-counter, forward-biased, loop-exit, misprediction-recovery, and dmem-stall training tests with RVFI PC-order checks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 193.29, \"delta_pct\": -40.29, \"vs_baseline\": -31.66, \"fmax_mhz\": 77.61, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4015113, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10146, \"ff\": 1963, \"seeds\": [81.83, 77.61, 77.21], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:34:05.456895Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 3}\n11:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Unsigned multiplier with sign correction\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The shared 33-by-33 signed multiplier substantially raised Fmax, showing\\nthat RV32M arithmetic cone size and routing dominate the current FPGA\\nimplementation. A 32-by-32 unsigned product is sufficient for every\\nmultiply variant when high-half signedness is corrected algebraically.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The shared 33-by-33 signed multiplier substantially raised Fmax, showing\\nthat RV32M arithmetic cone size and routing dominate the current FPGA\\nimplementation. A 32-by-32 unsigned product is sufficient for every\\nmultiply variant when high-half signedness is corrected algebraically.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the 33-bit signed multiply with one 32-by-32 unsigned product and\\nderive MULH and MULHSU high words by subtracting the required sign-extension\\ncorrection terms from that product's high half. MUL and MULHU use the same\\nproduct directly. This preserves exact single-cycle RV32M semantics and the\\nALTOPS branch, while shrinking the multiplier input width and removing its\\nsign-extension routing; the small conditional correction adders should be\\ncheaper than the wider signed multiplier on the Tang Nano fabric.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Use one unsigned 32-by-32 product and algebraic high-half sign corrections for MULH and MULHSU, retaining ALTOPS and all divide behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add adversarial signedness and boundary vectors proving all four multiply high/low result mappings against RV32M reference values.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Use one unsigned 32-by-32 product and algebraic high-half sign corrections for MULH and MULHSU, retaining ALTOPS and all divide behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add adversarial signedness and boundary vectors proving all four multiply high/low result mappings against RV32M reference values.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 355.89, \"delta_pct\": -1.56, \"vs_baseline\": 25.84, \"fmax_mhz\": 144.32, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4055214, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9893, \"ff\": 1922, \"seeds\": [144.32, 134.99, 147.19], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:41:35.419879Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (regression, -1.56%): On this Tang Nano RV32M datapath, replacing the shared signed multiplier with an unsigned product plus serial sign-correction subtractors regresses despite narrower inputs, so high-half correction logic and its control/routing can outweigh a one-bit multiplier-width reduction.\"}\n12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage JALR recovery\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"BTFNT already removes the common direct-JAL and backward-loop recovery\\nbubbles, but JALR still waits for EX to resolve its register target. CoreMark\\nreturns from frequently called helpers through JALR, so that fixed extra\\nstage costs IPC. The failed RAS attempt shows this benefit should not be\\nbought with more target-selection logic on the fetch critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"BTFNT already removes the common direct-JAL and backward-loop recovery\\nbubbles, but JALR still waits for EX to resolve its register target. CoreMark\\nreturns from frequently called helpers through JALR, so that fixed extra\\nstage costs IPC. The failed RAS attempt shows this benefit should not be\\nbought with more target-selection logic on the fetch critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Resolve dependency-free, aligned legal JALRs in ID using the existing\\nwrite-first regfile value and redirect fetch at the ID/EX boundary. Record\\nthe resolved target as that instruction's predicted successor so its normal\\nEX pass does not redirect again. If the immediately older EX instruction\\nwrites JALR's rs1, or the target is misaligned, defer to the present EX\\nresolver and trap path. EX redirects retain priority over an ID redirect,\\nensuring a wrong-path JALR is flushed. This removes one recovery cycle for\\nordinary returns without widening the IF prediction datapath or changing\\nretirement order.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize accepted dependency-free JALRs, compute their aligned rs1-plus-immediate target, emit an early redirect, and carry that target as pred_next_pc while retaining EX fallback cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish older EX recovery from an ID JALR redirect: flush IF for either source, but flush ID only for the older EX redirect so the redirecting JALR still advances once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Arbitrate EX-over-ID redirects, gate early JALR recovery on the absence of an immediately older rs1 producer and memory holds, and preserve the single-channel RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the arbitrated earlier redirect with the existing redirect-over-stall priority, emitting a bubble for the speculative fetch slot.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain the existing resolved-versus-predicted comparison so deferred or dependency-bound JALRs recover in EX and early-resolved JALRs cannot redirect twice.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add return-style JALR, ALU-to-JALR dependency fallback, load-to-JALR under dmem backpressure, misaligned-target trap, and simultaneous older-redirect tests with RVFI PC and order checks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize accepted dependency-free JALRs, compute their aligned rs1-plus-immediate target, emit an early redirect, and carry that target as pred_next_pc while retaining EX fallback cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish older EX recovery from an ID JALR redirect: flush IF for either source, but flush ID only for the older EX redirect so the redirecting JALR still advances once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Arbitrate EX-over-ID redirects, gate early JALR recovery on the absence of an immediately older rs1 producer and memory holds, and preserve the single-channel RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the arbitrated earlier redirect with the existing redirect-over-stall priority, emitting a bubble for the speculative fetch slot.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain the existing resolved-versus-predicted comparison so deferred or dependency-bound JALRs recover in EX and early-resolved JALRs cannot redirect twice.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add return-style JALR, ALU-to-JALR dependency fallback, load-to-JALR under dmem backpressure, misaligned-target trap, and simultaneous older-redirect tests with RVFI PC and order checks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 324.04, \"delta_pct\": -10.37, \"vs_baseline\": 14.57, \"fmax_mhz\": 130.96, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4041507, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10369, \"ff\": 1923, \"seeds\": [132.22, 130.96, 126.37], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:48:20.223113Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4}\n13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Opcode-biased static loop prediction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"BTFNT usefully predicts the usual backward counted-loop branch, but it\\ntreats every valid backward conditional branch alike.  CoreMark's loop\\ncontinuations are normally BNE/BLT/BLTU, whereas equality and greater-or-\\nequal branches are often inverted exit or guard tests; predicting those\\nbackward branches taken can introduce repeated recovery bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"BTFNT usefully predicts the usual backward counted-loop branch, but it\\ntreats every valid backward conditional branch alike.  CoreMark's loop\\ncontinuations are normally BNE/BLT/BLTU, whereas equality and greater-or-\\nequal branches are often inverted exit or guard tests; predicting those\\nbackward branches taken can introduce repeated recovery bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the existing stateless BTFNT predictor into an opcode-biased static\\npredictor.  Keep direct JAL prediction unchanged, and predict a backward\\nconditional branch taken only for the loop-continuation predicates\\nBNE, BLT, and BLTU; predict BEQ, BGE, and BGEU fall-through regardless of\\ndisplacement.  The predictor still derives targets solely from the current\\ninstruction immediate and uses the existing EX next-PC comparison for all\\nrecovery, so it adds neither tables nor predictor-state timing while\\navoiding systematic static-direction mistakes on inverted loop exits.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Make static conditional-branch direction depend on funct3 as well as backward displacement, retaining immediate-only target generation and the direct-JAL path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for backward BNE/BLT/BLTU taken predictions and backward BEQ/BGE/BGEU fall-through predictions, including EX recovery and RVFI PC sequencing.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Make static conditional-branch direction depend on funct3 as well as backward displacement, retaining immediate-only target generation and the direct-JAL path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for backward BNE/BLT/BLTU taken predictions and backward BEQ/BGE/BGEU fall-through predictions, including EX recovery and RVFI PC sequencing.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 365.65, \"delta_pct\": 1.14, \"vs_baseline\": 29.29, \"fmax_mhz\": 147.84, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4043217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9780, \"ff\": 1922, \"seeds\": [147.84, 153.0, 144.99], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:44:50.903850Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r4s2 (improvement, +1.14%): Refining stateless BTFNT to take backward BNE/BLT/BLTU but fall through BEQ/BGE/BGEU better matches CoreMark's counted-loop continuations versus inverted exits, avoiding recurring EX recovery bubbles without adding predictor state or a fetch-path table.\"}\n14:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Shared magnitude divide datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently describes distinct signed and unsigned quotient and\\nremainder operations.  Those four arithmetic expressions can infer\\nseparate large divider cones even though one RV32IM instruction uses only\\none result per cycle, adding routing pressure on the Tang Nano fabric.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently describes distinct signed and unsigned quotient and\\nremainder operations.  Those four arithmetic expressions can infer\\nseparate large divider cones even though one RV32IM instruction uses only\\none result per cycle, adding routing pressure on the Tang Nano fabric.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Canonicalize DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU operands into unsigned magnitudes,\\ncompute one shared 32-bit unsigned quotient/remainder pair, and restore\\nthe required signs for signed quotient and remainder results.  Directly\\nhandle divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 before selecting the shared result.\\nThis preserves exact one-cycle RV32M latency and the existing ALTOPS\\nbehavior while removing duplicated divide/modulo cones, so placement and\\nFmax can improve without a new pipeline stall mechanism.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"f