in the random-stall cosimulation.\n\nERROR: cosim_failed: coremark.elf\nLESSON: \n\nID: hyp-20260711-002-r11s1\nTITLE: MEM-stage completion and retirement\nOUTCOME: broken\nHYPOTHESIS: Collapse the MEM/WB stage into an acknowledged MEM completion boundary.\nRetire and write the register file directly from the EX/MEM instruction\nonce a load or store is accepted (or immediately for a non-memory op), and\nconstruct RVFI from that same completion snapshot. Keep only a narrow\nregistered writeback-bypass record for the one older producer needed by\nEX forwarding. This removes the wide MEM/WB state and its broad routing\nwhile keeping the zero-bubble load bypass, in-order retirement, and the\nexisting stall behavior; it should improve placement/Fmax without adding\nfetch-path logic or an IPC penalty.\n\nERROR: cosim_failed: selftest.elf\nLESSON: \n\nID: hyp-20260711-002-r12s1\nTITLE: Lean posted RAM store queue\nOUTCOME: placement_failed\nHYPOTHESIS: Add a one-entry posted-write latch only for ordinary RAM stores. An\naccepted, aligned RAM store retires when its final address/data/mask are\ncaptured, then the latch owns the unchanged dmem write port until\nio_dmemReady accepts it; it may drain and accept the next RAM store in the\nsame clock edge. Loads and all 0x10000000-0x10000200 MMIO accesses fence\nbehind a nonempty latch, and MMIO writes remain synchronous, preserving\nload ordering, UART output, and CoreMark timing-marker order. Keep the\nqueue entirely after MEM so its mux and state do not extend the proven\nfetch or EX critical cones.\n\nERROR: placement_failed\nLESSON: \n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
