{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n -i 'predict|btfnt|branch|jalr|ras|direction' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (improvement, +9.61%): Carrying IF's predicted next PC to EX lets a static BTFNT policy avoid recovery bubbles on CoreMark's frequent backward counted-loop branches (and direct JALs), making low-cost fetch-side control prediction an effective IPC optimization without a BTB.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): A zero-bubble MEM-to-EX load bypass must be qualified by the actual memory-response handshake (and freeze the consumer until then), since selecting combinational load data before acceptance corrupts dependent register results under dmem backpressure.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +4.42%): Once the MEM-to-EX load bypass is response-qualified and the pipeline holds on dmem backpressure, removing the load-use interlock recovers IPC on CoreMark's adjacent pointer-chasing and state-update load consumers without weakening in-order correctness.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, -8.15%): A RAS-top mux and canonical-return decode on the IF next-PC path can cost more timing than CoreMark gains from avoiding return recoveries, so return prediction must preserve the fetch critical path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (improvement, +11.68%): Feeding opcode-selected sign/zero-extended 33-bit operands into one signed multiplier preserves all RV32M high-half products while eliminating parallel signedness-specific multiply cones, reducing LUT/routing pressure enough to improve Tang Nano placement and Fmax.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (regression, -1.56%): On this Tang Nano RV32M datapath, replacing the shared signed multiplier with an unsigned product plus serial sign-correction subtractors regresses despite narrower inputs, so high-half correction logic and its control/routing can outweigh a one-bit multiplier-width reduction.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r4s2 (improvement, +1.14%): Refining stateless BTFNT to take backward BNE/BLT/BLTU but fall through BEQ/BGE/BGEU better matches CoreMark's counted-loop continuations versus inverted exits, avoiding recurring EX recovery bubbles without adding predictor state or a fetch-path table.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -73.49%): Sharing DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU through unsigned magnitude `/` and `%` expressions can sharply worsen Tang Nano timing because the operators remain separate divider cones while magnitude conversion and sign-restoration mux/negate logic lengthen the arithmetic path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 (regression, -3.50%): In CoreMark, backward BGE/BGEU executions can be biased taken yet still make worse static BTFNT candidates than BNE/BLT/BLTU, so a retired-direction profile alone cannot justify broadening fetch-local loop-continuation funct3 predicates.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (regression, -7.25%): On the Tang Nano RV32M datapath, splitting the shared 33-bit multiplier into four DSP-forced 17-by-17 signed partial products regresses because the widened signed partial-product recombination and extra multiplier placement/routing outweigh the benefit of smaller inferred multipliers.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (regression, -3.47%): Splitting EX/MEM and MEM/WB into lockstep execution and RVFI-trace records does not remove the trace state or its routing, so the added parallel interfaces and control can outweigh localized datapath narrowing on Tang Nano.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (regression, -7.66%): On the fetch-critical Tang Nano predictor path, selecting a 32-bit JAL/branch/sequential displacement before a shared PC adder puts a wide target mux ahead of carry propagation, so parallel PC-relative adders followed by target selection can place and time better despite redundant arithmetic.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (regression, -12.63%): Explicitly sharing 32-bit branch comparisons can worsen the timing-critical EX redirect cone when the shared relation nets and predicate mux add fanout and selection delay that direct per-funct3 relational expressions let synthesis optimize locally.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 (regression, -13.03%): For a small asynchronous-read register file, replacing data reset with a valid mask adds per-read valid gating and mask fanout on the decode-local read path, which can cost more timing and routing than eliminating reset on the data flops saves.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r7s2 (regression, -22.17%): On the fetch-critical Tang Nano next-PC cone, hand-minimizing the BNE/BLT/BLTU funct3 predicate can map and place worse than direct equality decodes despite identical branch behavior, so Boolean gate-count reduction alone is not a reliable timing optimization.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (improvement, +6.87%): Partitioning registered ALU control into an RV32I/RV32M class bit plus class-local opcode keeps CoreMark's ordinary arithmetic out of the combined base-plus-M result-selection mux, shortening the control-to-EX cone without changing execution semantics.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r8s2 (regression, -21.43%): Resolving source-ready conditional branches in ID puts two-source comparison, EX/MEM producer-scoreboarding, PC-target generation, and the ID-to-IF redirect mux on the front-end critical path, costing more timing than CoreMark recovers from a one-cycle-earlier misprediction fix.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r9s0 (improvement, +31.97%): Further partitioning RV32I ALU control into a coarse result class and class-local sub-operation keeps CoreMark's dominant ADD/SUB and AUIPC address-generation path out of logic, compare, shift, and LUI selection cones, substantially shortening the registered-control-to-EX routing/mux path on Tang Nano without changing latency.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 (regression, -11.98%): Moving a one-bit direction table to ID avoids fetch lookup but its PC-indexed hint, predicted-successor override, and EX/ID redirect arbitration still cost more than CoreMark gains from correcting the remaining forward/range branches after static BNE/BLT/BLTU loop prediction.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (regression, -43.09%): A one-word direct-mapped IF cache can lose badly when its tag compare, hit-data mux, and miss-wait/valid gating sit on the fetch-critical path (and each fill is bubbled), outweighing CoreMark hot-loop locality and avoided instruction-bus stalls.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (regression, -7.49%): CoreMark's backward BLT/BLTU branches remain valuable static loop-continuation predictions alongside BNE, so making them fall through reintroduces more EX recovery bubbles than it avoids from mispredicted range predicates.\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Iterative divider execution unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU unconditionally elaborates signed and unsigned divide and remainder\\ndatapaths, although CoreMark's timed kernels are dominated by loads, integer\\nALU operations, branches, and multiply-accumulate work. Four combinational\\ndivision/modulo expressions consume scarce LUT fabric and can set the slowest\\nALU route, limiting Fmax to 127 MHz near the LUT budget.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU unconditionally elaborates signed and unsigned divide and remainder\\ndatapaths, although CoreMark's timed kernels are dominated by loads, integer\\nALU operations, branches, and multiply-accumulate work. Four combinational\\ndivision/modulo expressions consume scarce LUT fabric and can set the slowest\\nALU route, limiting Fmax to 127 MHz near the LUT budget.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU implementation with one shared\\n32-cycle restoring divider that stalls the in-order execute path only for an\\nM divide/remainder instruction. Preserve the existing single-cycle path for\\nall other ALU and multiply operations. The rare divide latency should be\\noutside, or negligible within, the timed CoreMark kernel, while removing the\\nwide combinational divider should improve placement headroom and Fmax enough\\nto raise Fmax times CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the synthesised combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU datapaths while retaining the real and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS semantics for non-divide ALU operations.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/divider_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared signed/unsigned iterative restoring divider with request, busy, completion, quotient, and remainder interfaces, including all RV32M divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 corner cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact control metadata identifying the four divide/remainder ALU operations for execute-stage dispatch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Turn EX into a small execute sequencer: launch divider operations once, hold the instruction and redirect state while busy, and emit the completed quotient or remainder exactly once into EX/MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an execute-busy interlock that freezes younger IF/ID and ID/EX work without duplicating retirements or losing redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divider-busy control into the pipeline stall policy while preserving the existing RVFI retirement boundary and nret=1 IO contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Keep exact RV32M division and remainder edge-case coverage against the new functional-unit result path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed DIV/REM dependency and bus-stall tests to verify a single retirement, correct forwarding, and progress through the multi-cycle EX hold.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the synthesised combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU datapaths while retaining the real and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS semantics for non-divide ALU operations.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/divider_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared signed/unsigned iterative restoring divider with request, busy, completion, quotient, and remainder interfaces, including all RV32M divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 corner cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact control metadata identifying the four divide/remainder ALU operations for execute-stage dispatch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Turn EX into a small execute sequencer: launch divider operations once, hold the instruction and redirect state while busy, and emit the completed quotient or remainder exactly once into EX/MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an execute-busy interlock that freezes younger IF/ID and ID/EX work without duplicating retirements or losing redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divider-busy control into the pipeline stall policy while preserving the existing RVFI retirement boundary and nret=1 IO contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Keep exact RV32M division and remainder edge-case coverage against the new functional-unit result path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed DIV/REM dependency and bus-stall tests to verify a single retirement, correct forwarding, and progress through the multi-cycle EX hold.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1}\n4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Static loop branch prediction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The five-stage core always fetches PC+4 until EX resolves a branch or\\njump, so every taken loop back-edge discards useful fetch/decode work.\\nCoreMark contains many short counted loops, making this recurring control\\nhazard a material IPC limiter even with the zero-wait FPGA instruction bus.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The five-stage core always fetches PC+4 until EX resolves a branch or\\njump, so every taken loop back-edge discards useful fetch/decode work.\\nCoreMark contains many short counted loops, making this recurring control\\nhazard a material IPC limiter even with the zero-wait FPGA instruction bus.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a combinational static BTFNT predictor in IF: predict direct JALs and\\nbackward conditional branches taken, using their encoded PC-relative\\nimmediate, and otherwise predict fall-through. Carry the prediction with\\nthe instruction and have EX redirect and flush only on a misprediction;\\nJALR remains fall-through predicted. Correct loop-back predictions admit\\nthe target instruction into decode instead of paying the current recovery\\nbubble, while avoiding the LUT and timing cost of a BTB or dynamic table.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode direct control-flow immediates at fetch, select static BTFNT/JAL next PC, and accept EX recovery only for mispredictions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with prediction metadata needed to validate speculative fetch in EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch IF prediction metadata beside the decoded instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare predicted and resolved next PCs, producing a recovery redirect and flush only for control-flow mispredictions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire prediction metadata and misprediction recovery through the pipeline and hazard control.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace unconditional EX-control-flow flushing with misprediction-only flushing while retaining load and bus-stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed BTFNT, direct-JAL, fall-through, and misprediction-recovery tests including RVFI PC sequencing.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode direct control-flow immediates at fetch, select static BTFNT/JAL next PC, and accept EX recovery only for mispredictions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with prediction metadata needed to validate speculative fetch in EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch IF prediction metadata beside the decoded instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare predicted and resolved next PCs, producing a recovery redirect and flush only for control-flow mispredictions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire prediction metadata and misprediction recovery through the pipeline and hazard control.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace unconditional EX-control-flow flushing with misprediction-only flushing while retaining load and bus-stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed BTFNT, direct-JAL, fall-through, and misprediction-recovery tests including RVFI PC sequencing.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 310.0, \"delta_pct\": 9.61, \"vs_baseline\": 9.61, \"fmax_mhz\": 132.05, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10490, \"ff\": 1896, \"seeds\": [132.05, 135.98, 130.96], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:03:04.687018Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (improvement, +9.61%): Carrying IF's predicted next PC to EX lets a static BTFNT policy avoid recovery bubbles on CoreMark's frequent backward counted-loop branches (and direct JALs), making low-cost fetch-side control prediction an effective IPC optimization without a BTB.\"}\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-to-EX load forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline inserts a full bubble for every immediate\\nload consumer, even when the zero-wait data-memory response is already\\navailable combinationally during the load's MEM stage. CoreMark has many\\npointer-chasing and state-update sequences where this fixed interlock\\nreduces useful issue bandwidth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline inserts a full bubble for every immediate\\nload consumer, even when the zero-wait data-memory response is already\\navailable combinationally during the load's MEM stage. CoreMark has many\\npointer-chasing and state-update sequences where this fixed interlock\\nreduces useful issue bandwidth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a late MEM-to-EX bypass that forwards a load's fully extracted and\\nsign/zero-extended dmem result directly to the following instruction's EX\\noperand mux. Remove the normal load-use bubble; when io_dmemReady is low,\\nretain the existing whole-pipeline memory hold so no consumer observes an\\nunacknowledged load. This converts adjacent load-consumer pairs from two\\nexecution cycles to one in the common zero-wait case while preserving\\nin-order retirement, RVFI source values, and precise bus-stall behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the combinationally extracted, sign/zero-extended load value as a dedicated forwarding result alongside the existing MEM/WB capture path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish an EX/MEM load from an ALU producer so the youngest matching source selects the late load-data bypass rather than its address result.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the MEM-stage load-data input to the EX operand muxes and use it for immediately dependent ALU, store-data, branch, and JALR operands.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the fixed load-use bubble while retaining dmem-ready holds, redirect recovery, and all other pipeline interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the MEM load-result bypass and load classification through the forwarding network without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add adjacent load-to-ALU, load-to-store, load-to-branch, and random dmem-backpressure tests covering forwarding, stalls, and one retirement per instruction.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the combinationally extracted, sign/zero-extended load value as a dedicated forwarding result alongside the existing MEM/WB capture path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish an EX/MEM load from an ALU producer so the youngest matching source selects the late load-data bypass rather than its address result.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the MEM-stage load-data input to the EX operand muxes and use it for immediately dependent ALU, store-data, branch, and JALR operands.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the fixed load-use bubble while retaining dmem-ready holds, redirect recovery, and all other pipeline interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the MEM load-result bypass and load classification through the forwarding network without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add adjacent load-to-ALU, load-to-store, load-to-branch, and random dmem-backpressure tests covering forwarding, stalls, and one retirement per instruction.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 323.71, \"delta_pct\": 4.42, \"vs_baseline\": 14.46, \"fmax_mhz\": 131.27, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4055214, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10038, \"ff\": 1918, \"seeds\": [129.07, 131.27, 137.63], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:19:55.081660Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +4.42%): Once the MEM-to-EX load bypass is response-qualified and the pipeline holds on dmem backpressure, removing the load-use interlock recovers IPC on CoreMark's adjacent pointer-chasing and state-update load consumers without weakening in-order correctness.\"}\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Return-address stack prediction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Static BTFNT now predicts CoreMark loop back-edges and direct JAL calls,\\nbut conventional ABI returns still use JALR and therefore default to the\\nfall-through PC. Every frequently called CoreMark helper consequently pays\\nan EX-stage recovery despite its return address being statically available.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Static BTFNT now predicts CoreMark loop back-edges and direct JAL calls,\\nbut conventional ABI returns still use JALR and therefore default to the\\nfall-through PC. Every frequently called CoreMark helper consequently pays\\nan EX-stage recovery despite its return address being statically available.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a compact four-entry return-address stack. Decode pushes PC+4 for\\naccepted link-register calls and pops accepted canonical returns; IF uses\\nthe stack top to predict JALR x0, 0(x1/x5) as a return target. Keep the\\nexisting BTFNT/JAL policy for all other control flow and retain EX's\\npredicted-versus-resolved next-PC comparison as the single recovery path.\\nUpdating only accepted decode instructions prevents wrong-path calls or\\nreturns from corrupting predictor state while removing routine return\\nbubbles from the timed CoreMark call graph.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the RAS top and recognize canonical ABI return JALRs, selecting that target ahead of the sequential fallback.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Classify accepted JAL/JALR link-register calls and canonical returns, then emit compact RAS push/pop update signals only when the instruction is not flushed.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Add and wire a four-entry return-address stack whose state changes only for accepted decode instructions, preserving the existing nret=1 RVFI contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Continue validating the predicted successor against the resolved JALR target so empty, noncanonical, or stale RAS predictions recover exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed nested-call, return, empty-stack, and branch-flush-before-call tests, including RVFI PC sequencing through recovery.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the RAS top and recognize canonical ABI return JALRs, selecting that target ahead of the sequential fallback.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Classify accepted JAL/JALR link-register calls and canonical returns, then emit compact RAS push/pop update signals only when the instruction is not flushed.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Add and wire a four-entry return-address stack whose state changes only for accepted decode instructions, preserving the existing nret=1 RVFI contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Continue validating the predicted successor against the resolved JALR target so empty, noncanonical, or stale RAS predictions recover exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed nested-call, return, empty-stack, and branch-flush-before-call tests, including RVFI PC sequencing through recovery.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 284.75, \"delta_pct\": -8.15, \"vs_baseline\": 0.68, \"fmax_mhz\": 120.9, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4245805, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10868, \"ff\": 2024, \"seeds\": [120.9, 108.24, 125.03], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:17:42.013169Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, -8.15%): A RAS-top mux and canonical-return decode on the IF next-PC path can cost more timing than CoreMark gains from avoiding return recoveries, so return prediction must preserve the fetch critical path.\"}\n8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Shared sign-configurable multiplier\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently evaluates signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and\\nsigned-unsigned 64-bit products on every cycle, then selects one product\\nfor an RV32M multiply instruction. These parallel arithmetic cones consume\\nLUTs and routing even though only one product can retire per instruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently evaluates signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and\\nsigned-unsigned 64-bit products on every cycle, then selects one product\\nfor an RV32M multiply instruction. These parallel arithmetic cones consume\\nLUTs and routing even though only one product can retire per instruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three unconditional multiplier expressions with one 33-by-33\\nsigned multiplier whose operand extensions are selected from the ALU op:\\nsigned/signed for MULH, zero/zero for MULHU, and signed/zero for MULHSU.\\nMUL reads the shared product low word, which is identical for every signed\\ninterpretation. This preserves one-cycle exact RV32M multiply semantics and\\nthe formal ALTOPS branch, while reducing arithmetic duplication and routing\\npressure enough to improve Tang Nano placement and Fmax without adding a\\nstall or touching fetch prediction.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace three unconditional RV32M product datapaths with one sign-configurable 33-bit multiplier and select its architecturally correct low or high word.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized boundary vectors spanning MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU to prove the shared sign-extension mapping.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace three unconditional RV32M product datapaths with one sign-configurable 33-bit multiplier and select its architecturally correct low or high word.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized boundary vectors spanning MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU to prove the shared sign-extension mapping.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 361.53, \"delta_pct\": 11.68, \"vs_baseline\": 27.83, \"fmax_mhz\": 146.61, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4055214, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10581, \"ff\": 1922, \"seeds\": [147.71, 138.18, 146.61], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:31:08.004962Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (improvement, +11.68%): Feeding opcode-selected sign/zero-extended 33-bit operands into one signed multiplier preserves all RV32M high-half products while eliminating parallel signedness-specific multiply cones, reducing LUT/routing pressure enough to improve Tang Nano placement and Fmax.\"}\n10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny BTFNT-seeded branch direction table\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Static BTFNT already removes the common loop-back recovery bubble, but it\\ncannot learn the bias of forward conditional branches or branches whose\\ndirection differs from their displacement. CoreMark executes such tests in\\nits state machines and list-processing paths. The failed RAS shows that a\\npredictor must not add a wide target mux or substantial decode to fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Static BTFNT already removes the common loop-back recovery bubble, but it\\ncannot learn the bias of forward conditional branches or branches whose\\ndirection differs from their displacement. CoreMark executes such tests in\\nits state machines and list-processing paths. The failed RAS shows that a\\npredictor must not add a wide target mux or substantial decode to fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small direct-mapped, two-bit conditional-branch direction table that\\noverrides BTFNT only after that PC has been observed. On a cold entry, IF\\nkeeps the existing backward-taken/forward-not-taken decision and continues\\nto derive the target solely from the instruction immediate. EX trains the\\ntable with the resolved conditional-branch outcome exactly once when the\\ninstruction advances. This learns stable forward and atypical branch bias\\nwhile preserving the existing narrow fetch target path, direct-JAL policy,\\nrecovery mechanism, and precise behavior under memory stalls.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped valid-plus-two-bit direction table with combinational PC-indexed lookup, BTFNT-compatible cold entries, and one qualified EX-stage training port.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the predictor direction only for validated conditional branches, retaining immediate-only target formation, direct-JAL prediction, and BTFNT fallback on cold table entries.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the resolved conditional-branch PC and architectural direction as a training event only when EX advances, excluding stalled or trapped branch targets.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire the direction predictor between IF and EX without changing the nret=1 top-level or RVFI retirement interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed cold-start, saturating-counter, forward-biased, loop-exit, misprediction-recovery, and dmem-stall training tests with RVFI PC-order checks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped valid-plus-two-bit direction table with combinational PC-indexed lookup, BTFNT-compatible cold entries, and one qualified EX-stage training port.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the predictor direction only for validated conditional branches, retaining immediate-only target formation, direct-JAL prediction, and BTFNT fallback on cold table entries.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the resolved conditional-branch PC and architectural direction as a training event only when EX advances, excluding stalled or trapped branch targets.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire the direction predictor between IF and EX without changing the nret=1 top-level or RVFI retirement interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed cold-start, saturating-counter, forward-biased, loop-exit, misprediction-recovery, and dmem-stall training tests with RVFI PC-order checks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 193.29, \"delta_pct\": -40.29, \"vs_baseline\": -31.66, \"fmax_mhz\": 77.61, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4015113, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10146, \"ff\": 1963, \"seeds\": [81.83, 77.61, 77.21], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:34:05.456895Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 3}\n11:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Unsigned multiplier with sign correction\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The shared 33-by-33 signed multiplier substantially raised Fmax, showing\\nthat RV32M arithmetic cone size and routing dominate the current FPGA\\nimplementation. A 32-by-32 unsigned product is sufficient for every\\nmultiply variant when high-half signedness is corrected algebraically.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The shared 33-by-33 signed multiplier substantially raised Fmax, showing\\nthat RV32M arithmetic cone size and routing dominate the current FPGA\\nimplementation. A 32-by-32 unsigned product is sufficient for every\\nmultiply variant when high-half signedness is corrected algebraically.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the 33-bit signed multiply with one 32-by-32 unsigned product and\\nderive MULH and MULHSU high words by subtracting the required sign-extension\\ncorrection terms from that product's high half. MUL and MULHU use the same\\nproduct directly. This preserves exact single-cycle RV32M semantics and the\\nALTOPS branch, while shrinking the multiplier input width and removing its\\nsign-extension routing; the small conditional correction adders should be\\ncheaper than the wider signed multiplier on the Tang Nano fabric.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Use one unsigned 32-by-32 product and algebraic high-half sign corrections for MULH and MULHSU, retaining ALTOPS and all divide behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add adversarial signedness and boundary vectors proving all four multiply high/low result mappings against RV32M reference values.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Use one unsigned 32-by-32 product and algebraic high-half sign corrections for MULH and MULHSU, retaining ALTOPS and all divide behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add adversarial signedness and boundary vectors proving all four multiply high/low result mappings against RV32M reference values.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 355.89, \"delta_pct\": -1.56, \"vs_baseline\": 25.84, \"fmax_mhz\": 144.32, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4055214, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9893, \"ff\": 1922, \"seeds\": [144.32, 134.99, 147.19], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:41:35.419879Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (regression, -1.56%): On this Tang Nano RV32M datapath, replacing the shared signed multiplier with an unsigned product plus serial sign-correction subtractors regresses despite narrower inputs, so high-half correction logic and its control/routing can outweigh a one-bit multiplier-width reduction.\"}\n12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage JALR recovery\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"BTFNT already removes the common direct-JAL and backward-loop recovery\\nbubbles, but JALR still waits for EX to resolve its register target. CoreMark\\nreturns from frequently called helpers through JALR, so that fixed extra\\nstage costs IPC. The failed RAS attempt shows this benefit should not be\\nbought with more target-selection logic on the fetch critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"BTFNT already removes the common direct-JAL and backward-loop recovery\\nbubbles, but JALR still waits for EX to resolve its register target. CoreMark\\nreturns from frequently called helpers through JALR, so that fixed extra\\nstage costs IPC. The failed RAS attempt shows this benefit should not be\\nbought with more target-selection logic on the fetch critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Resolve dependency-free, aligned legal JALRs in ID using the existing\\nwrite-first regfile value and redirect fetch at the ID/EX boundary. Record\\nthe resolved target as that instruction's predicted successor so its normal\\nEX pass does not redirect again. If the immediately older EX instruction\\nwrites JALR's rs1, or the target is misaligned, defer to the present EX\\nresolver and trap path. EX redirects retain priority over an ID redirect,\\nensuring a wrong-path JALR is flushed. This removes one recovery cycle for\\nordinary returns without widening the IF prediction datapath or changing\\nretirement order.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize accepted dependency-free JALRs, compute their aligned rs1-plus-immediate target, emit an early redirect, and carry that target as pred_next_pc while retaining EX fallback cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish older EX recovery from an ID JALR redirect: flush IF for either source, but flush ID only for the older EX redirect so the redirecting JALR still advances once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Arbitrate EX-over-ID redirects, gate early JALR recovery on the absence of an immediately older rs1 producer and memory holds, and preserve the single-channel RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the arbitrated earlier redirect with the existing redirect-over-stall priority, emitting a bubble for the speculative fetch slot.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain the existing resolved-versus-predicted comparison so deferred or dependency-bound JALRs recover in EX and early-resolved JALRs cannot redirect twice.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add return-style JALR, ALU-to-JALR dependency fallback, load-to-JALR under dmem backpressure, misaligned-target trap, and simultaneous older-redirect tests with RVFI PC and order checks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize accepted dependency-free JALRs, compute their aligned rs1-plus-immediate target, emit an early redirect, and carry that target as pred_next_pc while retaining EX fallback cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish older EX recovery from an ID JALR redirect: flush IF for either source, but flush ID only for the older EX redirect so the redirecting JALR still advances once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Arbitrate EX-over-ID redirects, gate early JALR recovery on the absence of an immediately older rs1 producer and memory holds, and preserve the single-channel RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the arbitrated earlier redirect with the existing redirect-over-stall priority, emitting a bubble for the speculative fetch slot.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain the existing resolved-versus-predicted comparison so deferred or dependency-bound JALRs recover in EX and early-resolved JALRs cannot redirect twice.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add return-style JALR, ALU-to-JALR dependency fallback, load-to-JALR under dmem backpressure, misaligned-target trap, and simultaneous older-redirect tests with RVFI PC and order checks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 324.04, \"delta_pct\": -10.37, \"vs_baseline\": 14.57, \"fmax_mhz\": 130.96, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4041507, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10369, \"ff\": 1923, \"seeds\": [132.22, 130.96, 126.37], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:48:20.223113Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4}\n13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Opcode-biased static loop prediction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"BTFNT usefully predicts the usual backward counted-loop branch, but it\\ntreats every valid backward conditional branch alike.  CoreMark's loop\\ncontinuations are normally BNE/BLT/BLTU, whereas equality and greater-or-\\nequal branches are often inverted exit or guard tests; predicting those\\nbackward branches taken can introduce repeated recovery bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"BTFNT usefully predicts the usual backward counted-loop branch, but it\\ntreats every valid backward conditional branch alike.  CoreMark's loop\\ncontinuations are normally BNE/BLT/BLTU, whereas equality and greater-or-\\nequal branches are often inverted exit or guard tests; predicting those\\nbackward branches taken can introduce repeated recovery bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the existing stateless BTFNT predictor into an opcode-biased static\\npredictor.  Keep direct JAL prediction unchanged, and predict a backward\\nconditional branch taken only for the loop-continuation predicates\\nBNE, BLT, and BLTU; predict BEQ, BGE, and BGEU fall-through regardless of\\ndisplacement.  The predictor still derives targets solely from the current\\ninstruction immediate and uses the existing EX next-PC comparison for all\\nrecovery, so it adds neither tables nor predictor-state timing while\\navoiding systematic static-direction mistakes on inverted loop exits.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Make static conditional-branch direction depend on funct3 as well as backward displacement, retaining immediate-only target generation and the direct-JAL path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for backward BNE/BLT/BLTU taken predictions and backward BEQ/BGE/BGEU fall-through predictions, including EX recovery and RVFI PC sequencing.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Make static conditional-branch direction depend on funct3 as well as backward displacement, retaining immediate-only target generation and the direct-JAL path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for backward BNE/BLT/BLTU taken predictions and backward BEQ/BGE/BGEU fall-through predictions, including EX recovery and RVFI PC sequencing.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 365.65, \"delta_pct\": 1.14, \"vs_baseline\": 29.29, \"fmax_mhz\": 147.84, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4043217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9780, \"ff\": 1922, \"seeds\": [147.84, 153.0, 144.99], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:44:50.903850Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r4s2 (improvement, +1.14%): Refining stateless BTFNT to take backward BNE/BLT/BLTU but fall through BEQ/BGE/BGEU better matches CoreMark's counted-loop continuations versus inverted exits, avoiding recurring EX recovery bubbles without adding predictor state or a fetch-path table.\"}\n14:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Shared magnitude divide datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently describes distinct signed and unsigned quotient and\\nremainder operations.  Those four arithmetic expressions can infer\\nseparate large divider cones even though one RV32IM instruction uses only\\none result per cycle, adding routing pressure on the Tang Nano fabric.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently describes distinct signed and unsigned quotient and\\nremainder operations.  Those four arithmetic expressions can infer\\nseparate large divider cones even though one RV32IM instruction uses only\\none result per cycle, adding routing pressure on the Tang Nano fabric.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Canonicalize DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU operands into unsigned magnitudes,\\ncompute one shared 32-bit unsigned quotient/remainder pair, and restore\\nthe required signs for signed quotient and remainder results.  Directly\\nhandle divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 before selecting the shared result.\\nThis preserves exact one-cycle RV32M latency and the existing ALTOPS\\nbehavior while removing duplicated divide/modulo cones, so placement and\\nFmax can improve without a new pipeline stall mechanism.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the four real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU expressions with one unsigned magnitude div/mod pair, signed-result restoration, and exact RV32M corner-case selects while retaining the ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add randomized and boundary coverage proving shared-path quotient and remainder signs, divide-by-zero behavior, and INT_MIN/-1 results for all four divide operations.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the four real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU expressions with one unsigned magnitude div/mod pair, signed-result restoration, and exact RV32M corner-case selects while retaining the ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add randomized and boundary coverage proving shared-path quotient and remainder signs, divide-by-zero behavior, and INT_MIN/-1 results for all four divide operations.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 96.93, \"delta_pct\": -73.49, \"vs_baseline\": -65.73, \"fmax_mhz\": 39.19, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4043217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 7706, \"ff\": 1918, \"seeds\": [40.52, 37.86, 39.19], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:58:29.535556Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -73.49%): Sharing DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU through unsigned magnitude `/` and `%` expressions can sharply worsen Tang Nano timing because the operators remain separate divider cones while magnitude conversion and sign-restoration mux/negate logic lengthen the arithmetic path.\"}\n16:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Profiled backward range-loop prediction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current opcode-biased static predictor correctly takes backward BNE, BLT,\\nand BLTU loop continuations, but falls through on every backward BGE and BGEU.\\nA CoreMark retired-instruction profile shows 6,650 backward BGE and 16,320\\nbackward BGEU executions in the timed region, all taken, so these choices\\nstill incur repeated EX recovery bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current opcode-biased static predictor correctly takes backward BNE, BLT,\\nand BLTU loop continuations, but falls through on every backward BGE and BGEU.\\nA CoreMark retired-instruction profile shows 6,650 backward BGE and 16,320\\nbackward BGEU executions in the timed region, all taken, so these choices\\nstill incur repeated EX recovery bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the existing stateless backward-loop predicate to include BGE and\\nBGEU, while retaining fall-through prediction for BEQ and preserving the\\ndirect-JAL path. This is a fetch-local funct3 comparison change: targets are\\nstill formed only from the encoded immediate and EX remains the sole\\narchitectural resolver. It recovers the profiled range-loop back edges\\nwithout adding predictor state, tag RAMs, training logic, or another wide\\nnext-PC mux to the timing-critical fetch path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Include backward BGE and BGEU in the stateless taken-loop predicate, retaining direct-JAL prediction, immediate-only targets, and BEQ fall-through.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed backward BGE and BGEU taken-prediction cases plus loop-exit recovery and RVFI PC-order checks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Include backward BGE and BGEU in the stateless taken-loop predicate, retaining direct-JAL prediction, immediate-only targets, and BEQ fall-through.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed backward BGE and BGEU taken-prediction cases plus loop-exit recovery and RVFI PC-order checks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 352.87, \"delta_pct\": -3.5, \"vs_baseline\": 24.77, \"fmax_mhz\": 142.13, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4027817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9984, \"ff\": 1918, \"seeds\": [133.99, 142.13, 149.45], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T01:55:31.310215Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 (regression, -3.50%): In CoreMark, backward BGE/BGEU executions can be biased taken yet still make worse static BTFNT candidates than BNE/BLT/BLTU, so a retired-direction profile alone cannot justify broadening fetch-local loop-continuation funct3 predicates.\"}\n18:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Narrow execution and trace pipes\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX/MEM and MEM/WB bundles carry every RVFI-only source value,\\nPC field, branch field, and memory-observation field beside the live\\nexecute datapath. Those wide bundled registers and their broad module\\ninterfaces increase routing pressure around the ALU, forwarding muxes, and\\nmemory stage even though most fields do not affect forward progress.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX/MEM and MEM/WB bundles carry every RVFI-only source value,\\nPC field, branch field, and memory-observation field beside the live\\nexecute datapath. Those wide bundled registers and their broad module\\ninterfaces increase routing pressure around the ALU, forwarding muxes, and\\nmemory stage even though most fields do not affect forward progress.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Partition the pipeline into a narrow execution pipe and a lockstep\\nretirement-trace pipe. The execution pipe keeps only data and control that\\nthe following stage consumes for architectural execution, while a sideband\\ntrace record carries RVFI-only operand, PC, instruction, and memory\\nobservations to the same single retirement boundary. Both pipes use the\\nidentical valid/hold/flush enables, so retirement order and every RVFI\\nvalue remain unchanged. Reducing wide high-fanout execute-stage bundles\\nshould improve Tang Nano placement and Fmax without adding an IPC penalty.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Split the existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB payload definitions into compact execution records and lockstep RVFI trace records, keeping all architectural fields represented exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Register only live ALU, store-data, destination, control, and valid state on the execution path while emitting a separately registered trace record containing post-forward source observations and resolved PC metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrow execution record for the memory transaction, update the trace record with aligned-memory masks and raw read/write observations, and retain identical hold behavior for both records.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select writeback data from the compact retirement execution record without depending on RVFI-only trace fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the paired records through forwarding and retirement, constructing channel-0 RVFI exclusively from the trace record plus the unchanged writeback result.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add equivalence-oriented pipeline cases covering ALU, load, store, branch, redirect, and random bus stalls, checking unchanged RVFI operands, memory fields, order, and single retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Split the existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB payload definitions into compact execution records and lockstep RVFI trace records, keeping all architectural fields represented exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Register only live ALU, store-data, destination, control, and valid state on the execution path while emitting a separately registered trace record containing post-forward source observations and resolved PC metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrow execution record for the memory transaction, update the trace record with aligned-memory masks and raw read/write observations, and retain identical hold behavior for both records.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select writeback data from the compact retirement execution record without depending on RVFI-only trace fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the paired records through forwarding and retirement, constructing channel-0 RVFI exclusively from the trace record plus the unchanged writeback result.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add equivalence-oriented pipeline cases covering ALU, load, store, branch, redirect, and random bus stalls, checking unchanged RVFI operands, memory fields, order, and single retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 352.96, \"delta_pct\": -3.47, \"vs_baseline\": 24.8, \"fmax_mhz\": 142.71, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4043217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10324, \"ff\": 1922, \"seeds\": [148.3, 141.7, 142.71], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T02:16:23.649583Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (regression, -3.47%): Splitting EX/MEM and MEM/WB into lockstep execution and RVFI-trace records does not remove the trace state or its routing, so the added parallel interfaces and control can outweigh localized datapath narrowing on Tang Nano.\"}\n19:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Shared static predictor target adder\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current successful static predictor forms PC+4, PC+JAL-immediate,\\nand PC+branch-immediate in parallel before selecting one result. Those\\nredundant 32-bit adders and their routing sit in the fetch next-PC cone,\\ndespite only one successor ever being required each cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current successful static predictor forms PC+4, PC+JAL-immediate,\\nand PC+branch-immediate in parallel before selecting one result. Those\\nredundant 32-bit adders and their routing sit in the fetch next-PC cone,\\ndespite only one successor ever being required each cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Preserve the current direct-JAL and opcode-biased backward BNE/BLT/BLTU\\nprediction policy, but first select a single predicted displacement\\n(four, JAL immediate, or branch immediate) and then compute the successor\\nwith one shared PC adder. This retains identical predicted PCs, EX\\nrecovery, and CoreMark IPC while reducing LUT use and routing pressure in\\nthe timing-sensitive fetch predictor; unlike the failed BHT and RAS, it\\nadds no predictor state, tags, or target mux after a wide arithmetic cone.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor the static JAL and opcode-biased loop predictor to select one immediate displacement before one shared next-PC adder, preserving all existing prediction decisions.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add equivalence-oriented predictor tests for direct JAL, backward BNE/BLT/BLTU, fall-through branch classes, redirects, and RVFI PC sequencing.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor the static JAL and opcode-biased loop predictor to select one immediate displacement before one shared next-PC adder, preserving all existing prediction decisions.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add equivalence-oriented predictor tests for direct JAL, backward BNE/BLT/BLTU, fall-through branch classes, redirects, and RVFI PC sequencing.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 337.65, \"delta_pct\": -7.66, \"vs_baseline\": 19.39, \"fmax_mhz\": 136.52, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4043217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10205, \"ff\": 1922, \"seeds\": [142.94, 136.52, 136.44], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T02:11:33.325304Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (regression, -7.66%): On the fetch-critical Tang Nano predictor path, selecting a 32-bit JAL/branch/sequential displacement before a shared PC adder puts a wide target mux ahead of carry propagation, so parallel PC-relative adders followed by target selection can place and time better despite redundant arithmetic.\"}\n20:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Shared EX branch comparison primitives\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark executes many conditional loop branches, so the EX compare and\\nredirect cone is both frequently active and timing sensitive. The current\\nbranch case describes six independent 32-bit relation expressions even\\nthough the six RV32I predicates are derivable from only equality, signed\\nless-than, and unsigned less-than results.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark executes many conditional loop branches, so the EX compare and\\nredirect cone is both frequently active and timing sensitive. The current\\nbranch case describes six independent 32-bit relation expressions even\\nthough the six RV32I predicates are derivable from only equality, signed\\nless-than, and unsigned less-than results.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor EX branch resolution to compute three shared compare primitives\\nonce per instruction: rs1==rs2, signed rs1<rs2, and unsigned rs1<rs2.\\nSelect BEQ/BNE directly or by equality inversion, and form BGE/BGEU by\\ninverting the appropriate less-than primitive. This preserves every branch\\ndecision and prediction-recovery behavior, while removing duplicate wide\\ncomparator cones and reducing routing into the branch-condition mux.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the six-operation branch comparator case with shared equality, signed-less-than, and unsigned-less-than primitives plus predicate inversion/select logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed signed and unsigned branch boundary cases for all six predicates, including taken and fall-through recovery with RVFI PC sequencing.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the six-operation branch comparator case with shared equality, signed-less-than, and unsigned-less-than primitives plus predicate inversion/select logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed signed and unsigned branch boundary cases for all six predicates, including taken and fall-through recovery with RVFI PC sequencing.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 319.47, \"delta_pct\": -12.63, \"vs_baseline\": 12.96, \"fmax_mhz\": 129.17, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4043217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10235, \"ff\": 1922, \"seeds\": [128.9, 129.17, 133.05], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T02:26:05.997606Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (regression, -12.63%): Explicitly sharing 32-bit branch comparisons can worsen the timing-critical EX redirect cone when the shared relation nets and predicate mux add fanout and selection delay that direct per-funct3 relational expressions let synthesis optimize locally.\"}\n22:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Minimized static loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The best CoreMark result uses a stateless fetch-local policy, but its\\nBNE/BLT/BLTU selector is currently expressed as several three-bit equality\\ncomparisons plus a redundant branch-validity check in the next-PC cone.\\nThis cone is timing-sensitive on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best CoreMark result uses a stateless fetch-local policy, but its\\nBNE/BLT/BLTU selector is currently expressed as several three-bit equality\\ncomparisons plus a redundant branch-validity check in the next-PC cone.\\nThis cone is timing-sensitive on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Preserve exactly the current direct-JAL and backward BNE/BLT/BLTU prediction\\nbehavior, but replace the loop-continuation decode with its minimized Boolean\\nform and remove the redundant reserved-branch validation from that predicate.\\nThe minimized predicate is true only for funct3 values 001, 100, and 110;\\nit therefore cannot predict any reserved branch encoding. This reduces LUT\\ndepth and fanout before the existing parallel target adders and target mux,\\nretaining the proven IPC benefit without adding state or changing recovery.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the equality-chain loop-continuation decode with an exact minimized BNE/BLT/BLTU Boolean predicate while retaining the existing parallel PC-relative adders and prediction semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add equivalence-oriented directed cases proving the minimized fetch predicate predicts only backward BNE, BLT, and BLTU, including reserved funct3 fall-through and RVFI PC recovery.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the equality-chain loop-continuation decode with an exact minimized BNE/BLT/BLTU Boolean predicate while retaining the existing parallel PC-relative adders and prediction semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add equivalence-oriented directed cases proving the minimized fetch predicate predicts only backward BNE, BLT, and BLTU, including reserved funct3 fall-through and RVFI PC recovery.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 284.58, \"delta_pct\": -22.17, \"vs_baseline\": 0.62, \"fmax_mhz\": 115.06, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4043217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10028, \"ff\": 1922, \"seeds\": [115.06, 122.53, 112.08], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T02:24:09.769271Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r7s2 (regression, -22.17%): On the fetch-critical Tang Nano next-PC cone, hand-minimizing the BNE/BLT/BLTU funct3 predicate can map and place worse than direct equality decodes despite identical branch behavior, so Boolean gate-count reduction alone is not a reliable timing optimization.\"}\n23:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Class-partitioned ALU control\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The routed timing reports show an ID/EX control bit entering the long EX\\nresult-selection cone. The present five-bit sequential ALU opcode makes one\\nflat case cover base integer operations plus all eight RV32M operations,\\ncreating a broad binary-controlled output selection network even when the\\ntimed CoreMark kernel is executing ordinary arithmetic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The routed timing reports show an ID/EX control bit entering the long EX\\nresult-selection cone. The present five-bit sequential ALU opcode makes one\\nflat case cover base integer operations plus all eight RV32M operations,\\ncreating a broad binary-controlled output selection network even when the\\ntimed CoreMark kernel is executing ordinary arithmetic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Re-encode the registered ALU control as an explicit RV32M class bit plus a\\ncompact operation code local to that class. In alu.sv, use the class bit as\\nthe first-level split: the base RV32I/LUI path selects only base results,\\nwhile the M path selects multiply/divide/remainder results and retains the\\nexact RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS alternatives. This keeps the same one-cycle\\nsemantics, forwarding, and pipeline timing, but partitions the wide EX\\nresult mux so ordinary ALU control need not traverse the combined 19-way\\nopcode selection cone. It specifically attacks the observed control-to-EX\\ncritical path without changing fetch prediction or adding state.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the flat ALU-operation field in ctrl_t with a compact base/M class encoding and class-local operation constants while preserving all control-bundle semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the class-partitioned ALU control only for validated instruction encodings, retaining the illegal-default discipline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the decoder's class and class-local ALU operation fields into ID/EX without altering decode, valid, or flush behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the class-partitioned control fields to the ALU while preserving existing operand forwarding, branch resolution, and EX/MEM state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Split base and RV32M result selection at a first-level class branch, preserving exact RV32M corner cases and matching ALTOPS formulas.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend directed coverage across every base and M control class, including multiply signedness and division corner cases.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the flat ALU-operation field in ctrl_t with a compact base/M class encoding and class-local operation constants while preserving all control-bundle semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the class-partitioned ALU control only for validated instruction encodings, retaining the illegal-default discipline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the decoder's class and class-local ALU operation fields into ID/EX without altering decode, valid, or flush behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the class-partitioned control fields to the ALU while preserving existing operand forwarding, branch resolution, and EX/MEM state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Split base and RV32M result selection at a first-level class branch, preserving exact RV32M corner cases and matching ALTOPS formulas.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend directed coverage across every base and M control class, including multiply signedness and division corner cases.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 390.78, \"delta_pct\": 6.87, \"vs_baseline\": 38.17, \"fmax_mhz\": 158.0, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4043217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10620, \"ff\": 1922, \"seeds\": [162.23, 140.51, 158.0], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T02:40:03.693737Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (improvement, +6.87%): Partitioning registered ALU control into an RV32I/RV32M class bit plus class-local opcode keeps CoreMark's ordinary arithmetic out of the combined base-plus-M result-selection mux, shortening the control-to-EX cone without changing execution semantics.\"}\n24:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic instruction fetch queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current frontend stops its PC and discards each fetch opportunity whenever\\na data-memory transaction stalls the in-order pipeline.  The independent\\ninstruction and data buses in the benchmark permit useful instruction fetch\\nduring those holds, creating a reserve that can absorb later random imem\\nbackpressure without changing architectural issue order.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current frontend stops its PC and discards each fetch opportunity whenever\\na data-memory transaction stalls the in-order pipeline.  The independent\\ninstruction and data buses in the benchmark permit useful instruction fetch\\nduring those holds, creating a reserve that can absorb later random imem\\nbackpressure without changing architectural issue order.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF-to-ID handoff with a compact two-entry elastic\\nfetch queue.  Fetch continues into an empty queue slot while MEM is held for\\ndmem backpressure, and ID consumes queued entries only when it can advance.\\nEach entry carries PC, instruction, and the existing predicted successor;\\nan EX redirect flushes all speculative entries and restarts fetch at the\\nresolved target.  This preserves the proven static prediction policy and\\nsingle-retire pipeline while overlapping independent imem service with data\\nstalls, improving CoreMark IPC in the random-stall cosimulation.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a two-entry valid/ready fetch queue that records PC, instruction, and predicted successor, permits fetch during downstream holds, and flushes precisely on EX redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the direct IF/ID handoff wiring with fetch enqueue/dequeue control and preserve redirect-over-stall priority at the frontend boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate frontend queue consumption from dmem pipeline holding so a full or flushed queue backpressures fetch without allowing younger instructions into held execute state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume only a valid queue head when ID/EX can advance, retaining bubble, flush, and RVFI source-observation semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and random imem/dmem-stall tests for queue fill and drain, redirect flushing, branch prediction recovery, RVFI order, and no duplicate retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a two-entry valid/ready fetch queue that records PC, instruction, and predicted successor, permits fetch during downstream holds, and flushes precisely on EX redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the direct IF/ID handoff wiring with fetch enqueue/dequeue control and preserve redirect-over-stall priority at the frontend boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate frontend queue consumption from dmem pipeline holding so a full or flushed queue backpressures fetch without allowing younger instructions into held execute state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume only a valid queue head when ID/EX can advance, retaining bubble, flush, and RVFI source-observation semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and random imem/dmem-stall tests for queue fill and drain, redirect flushing, branch prediction recovery, RVFI order, and no duplicate retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: coremark.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 8}\n25:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage branch correction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The opcode-biased static predictor removes most loop back-edge bubbles, but\\nits intentional fall-through choices and final loop exits still recover only\\nafter EX. CoreMark executes many such conditional branches, so moving safe\\nmisprediction correction one stage earlier can raise IPC without a fetch\\ntable, RAS, or extra next-PC target-selection cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The opcode-biased static predictor removes most loop back-edge bubbles, but\\nits intentional fall-through choices and final loop exits still recover only\\nafter EX. CoreMark executes many such conditional branches, so moving safe\\nmisprediction correction one stage earlier can raise IPC without a fetch\\ntable, RAS, or extra next-PC target-selection cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a source-ready decode-stage branch resolver that validates only\\nconditional-branch predictions. When neither branch source is pending from\\nan older EX or MEM producer, ID compares the write-first regfile operands,\\nforms the existing PC-relative target, and redirects IF immediately on a\\npredicted-versus-actual mismatch. Carry the corrected successor as the\\nbranch's prediction so EX does not redirect a second time. If either source\\nis pending, a memory hold is active, or the branch target is misaligned, ID\\ndefers unchanged to the existing EX resolver. This retains the proven\\nstateless fetch policy and precise RVFI behavior while reducing the recovery\\npenalty of independent branch misses by one cycle.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add source-ready conditional-branch comparison, immediate target generation, and an early correction signal while preserving the normal ID/EX payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect older branch-source producers, arbitrate EX recovery over ID correction, and pass the corrected predicted successor into the pipeline without changing the top-level IO.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Handle an ID-only correction by flushing the fetched slot while allowing the correcting branch to advance, with EX recovery and dmem-hold priority retained.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the arbitrated earlier correction through the existing redirect-over-stall PC update path and emit the required speculative-fetch bubble.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Continue exact branch resolution and use the corrected ID successor as prediction metadata, preserving the EX fallback for dependencies and traps.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add independent branch-miss, ALU/load-dependent fallback, dmem-stalled, simultaneous EX-redirect, and RVFI PC/order directed tests.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add source-ready conditional-branch comparison, immediate target generation, and an early correction signal while preserving the normal ID/EX payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect older branch-source producers, arbitrate EX recovery over ID correction, and pass the corrected predicted successor into the pipeline without changing the top-level IO.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Handle an ID-only correction by flushing the fetched slot while allowing the correcting branch to advance, with EX recovery and dmem-hold priority retained.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the arbitrated earlier correction through the existing redirect-over-stall PC update path and emit the required speculative-fetch bubble.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Continue exact branch resolution and use the corrected ID successor as prediction metadata, preserving the EX fallback for dependencies and traps.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add independent branch-miss, ALU/load-dependent fallback, dmem-stalled, simultaneous EX-redirect, and RVFI PC/order directed tests.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 287.28, \"delta_pct\": -21.43, \"vs_baseline\": 1.58, \"fmax_mhz\": 115.05, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4004858, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10478, \"ff\": 1902, \"seeds\": [112.68, 115.05, 117.65], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T02:44:00.400249Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r8s2 (regression, -21.43%): Resolving source-ready conditional branches in ID puts two-source comparison, EX/MEM producer-scoreboarding, PC-target generation, and the ID-to-IF redirect mux on the front-end critical path, costing more timing than CoreMark recovers from a one-cycle-earlier misprediction fix.\"}\n26:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Fine-grained base ALU partition\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The class-partitioned ALU control raised fitness by keeping ordinary RV32I\\nexecution out of the RV32M result mux, but the base-class path still selects\\namong ADD/SUB, logic, compare, shift, and LUI results with one four-bit\\nopcode. CoreMark's hot address-generation and integer-update operations are\\noverwhelmingly ADD-class, so that remaining broad base selection is a likely\\ncontrol-to-EX routing and mux cost on the Tang Nano.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The class-partitioned ALU control raised fitness by keeping ordinary RV32I\\nexecution out of the RV32M result mux, but the base-class path still selects\\namong ADD/SUB, logic, compare, shift, and LUI results with one four-bit\\nopcode. CoreMark's hot address-generation and integer-update operations are\\noverwhelmingly ADD-class, so that remaining broad base selection is a likely\\ncontrol-to-EX routing and mux cost on the Tang Nano.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine only the base ALU encoding into a coarse result class plus a local\\nsub-operation. Give ADD/SUB and AUIPC address-generation a dedicated class\\nso their output is selected before the logic, compare, shift, and LUI cones;\\nthe local sub-operation is consumed only inside its selected class. Retain\\nthe existing RV32M class split, all ALU results, forwarding, and single-cycle\\nlatency. This extends the proven class-first mux partitioning to the common\\nRV32I path, aiming to shorten its registered-control-to-ALU-result cone\\nwithout adding frontend state or changing CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the flat base ALU operation encoding with compact base result-class and class-local sub-operation fields while retaining the RV32M class encoding.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the refined base result class only in validated opcode and funct matches, preserving the default-illegal discipline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the refined base ALU control through the ID/EX payload without changing valid, flush, or operand capture behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the refined base ALU control to execute while preserving forwarding, branch resolution, and EX/MEM timing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Split the base result mux by coarse ADD, logic, compare, shift, and pass-through classes, with sub-operation selection local to each class and unchanged RV32M handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add control-class boundary vectors covering every RV32I base result class, AUIPC/LUI routing, shifts, comparisons, and RV32M non-regression.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the flat base ALU operation encoding with compact base result-class and class-local sub-operation fields while retaining the RV32M class encoding.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the refined base result class only in validated opcode and funct matches, preserving the default-illegal discipline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the refined base ALU control through the ID/EX payload without changing valid, flush, or operand capture behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the refined base ALU control to execute while preserving forwarding, branch resolution, and EX/MEM timing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Split the base result mux by coarse ADD, logic, compare, shift, and pass-through classes, with sub-operation selection local to each class and unchanged RV32M handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add control-class boundary vectors covering every RV32I base result class, AUIPC/LUI routing, shifts, comparisons, and RV32M non-regression.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 515.7, \"delta_pct\": 31.97, \"vs_baseline\": 82.34, \"fmax_mhz\": 208.51, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4043217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10525, \"ff\": 1926, \"seeds\": [217.91, 200.12, 208.51], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:01:14.156609Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r9s0 (improvement, +31.97%): Further partitioning RV32I ALU control into a coarse result class and class-local sub-operation keeps CoreMark's dominant ADD/SUB and AUIPC address-generation path out of logic, compare, shift, and LUI selection cones, substantially shortening the registered-control-to-EX routing/mux path on Tang Nano without changing latency.\"}\n27:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Decoupled two-entry prefetch buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Random instruction and data bus stalls currently stop the shared frontend,\\neven when the independent instruction port could have accepted useful work.\\nCoreMark's in-order backend needs only one instruction per cycle, so a small\\nbuffer can turn independent service opportunities into tolerance for later\\ninstruction-memory backpressure without widening the predictor datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Random instruction and data bus stalls currently stop the shared frontend,\\neven when the independent instruction port could have accepted useful work.\\nCoreMark's in-order backend needs only one instruction per cycle, so a small\\nbuffer can turn independent service opportunities into tolerance for later\\ninstruction-memory backpressure without widening the predictor datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF-to-ID handoff with a two-entry, explicitly\\nenqueue/dequeue-controlled prefetch buffer.  An imem word is enqueued only\\non io_imemReady, and fetch may fill a vacant slot while ID/EX is held by a\\ndmem stall; ID dequeues only when its register advances.  Each entry carries\\nits PC, instruction, and already-computed predicted successor, keeping the\\nexisting static predictor off the queue-control critical path.  An EX\\nredirect discards every unconsumed speculative entry and restarts fetch from\\nthe resolved PC, while the older redirecting instruction proceeds normally.\\nThis is a narrow frontend elasticity change that preserves in-order issue,\\nRVFI retirement, and the proven branch policy while covering a portion of\\nthe independently injected imem stalls.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement an accepted-request two-entry prefetch buffer whose entries retain PC, instruction, and the existing predicted next PC; flush unconsumed entries atomically on EX redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire frontend enqueue/dequeue availability separately from backend advance, retaining redirect-over-stall PC priority and the unchanged external imem interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace direct IF/ID stall assumptions with queue backpressure and dequeue gating, while preserving whole-pipeline dmem holds and redirect flush priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch only a valid queue-head entry when decode is permitted to advance, preserving reset, flush, operand-read, and RVFI-source semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized imem/dmem-stall coverage for queue fill and drain, accepted-request accounting, redirect flushes, prediction recovery, and strict single-retirement RVFI order.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement an accepted-request two-entry prefetch buffer whose entries retain PC, instruction, and the existing predicted next PC; flush unconsumed entries atomically on EX redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire frontend enqueue/dequeue availability separately from backend advance, retaining redirect-over-stall PC priority and the unchanged external imem interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace direct IF/ID stall assumptions with queue backpressure and dequeue gating, while preserving whole-pipeline dmem holds and redirect flush priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch only a valid queue-head entry when decode is permitted to advance, preserving reset, flush, operand-read, and RVFI-source semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized imem/dmem-stall coverage for queue fill and drain, accepted-request accounting, redirect flushes, prediction recovery, and strict single-retirement RVFI order.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 150.33, \"delta_pct\": -61.53, \"vs_baseline\": -46.85, \"fmax_mhz\": 60.53, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4026396, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10425, \"ff\": 2110, \"seeds\": [60.53, 63.71, 58.72], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T02:57:24.036037Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 9}\n28:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Decode-late branch direction override\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The stateless IF predictor correctly handles direct JALs and the dominant\\nbackward BNE/BLT/BLTU loops, but it deliberately falls through for other\\nbranches. A conventional BHT improved neither fitness nor timing because\\nits lookup became part of the instruction-to-next-PC fetch critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The stateless IF predictor correctly handles direct JALs and the dominant\\nbackward BNE/BLT/BLTU loops, but it deliberately falls through for other\\nbranches. A conventional BHT improved neither fitness nor timing because\\nits lookup became part of the instruction-to-next-PC fetch critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny reset-to-not-taken, direct-mapped one-bit direction table, but\\nlook it up only once a conditional branch reaches ID. The existing IF policy\\nremains exactly unchanged. For a branch that IF predicted fall-through, a\\ntrained taken bit causes ID to redirect the PC to the already-encoded\\nPC-relative target and replaces the branch's carried predicted successor\\nwith that target. EX remains the sole architectural resolver and repairs a\\nfalse taken hint. Train only when an unstalled, non-trapping branch advances\\nfrom EX. This learns repeatedly taken forward/range branches one stage\\nbefore EX without putting table lookup, tag comparison, or a new target mux\\ninto the fetch next-PC cone; unlike the failed decode resolver, it performs\\nno register comparison, forwarding scoreboarding, or source-read logic.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_direction_table.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small PC-indexed one-bit branch-direction table with reset-to-not-taken state, combinational ID lookup, and a qualified EX training port.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize valid conditional branches that IF predicted fall-through, consume the table hint, form the existing PC-relative target, and carry an overridden predicted successor into ID/EX on a late taken prediction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit one qualified conditional-branch training event only when EX advances, while retaining EX as the exact branch and misprediction resolver.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the direction table, arbitrate EX recovery ahead of the ID late-taken redirect, and preserve redirect-over-stall priority without changing the nret=1 or RVFI IO contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish EX recovery, which flushes the younger fetched instruction, from an ID late redirect, where the correcting branch itself must still advance.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the arbitrated late redirect through the existing PC-update interface while retaining the current static direct-JAL and backward BNE/BLT/BLTU fetch policy unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add cold-start, trained forward-taken, alias, false-taken recovery, dmem-stalled training, and RVFI PC/order tests for the decode-late direction override.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_direction_table.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small PC-indexed one-bit branch-direction table with reset-to-not-taken state, combinational ID lookup, and a qualified EX training port.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize valid conditional branches that IF predicted fall-through, consume the table hint, form the existing PC-relative target, and carry an overridden predicted successor into ID/EX on a late taken prediction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit one qualified conditional-branch training event only when EX advances, while retaining EX as the exact branch and misprediction resolver.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the direction table, arbitrate EX recovery ahead of the ID late-taken redirect, and preserve redirect-over-stall priority without changing the nret=1 or RVFI IO contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish EX recovery, which flushes the younger fetched instruction, from an ID late redirect, where the correcting branch itself must still advance.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the arbitrated late redirect through the existing PC-update interface while retaining the current static direct-JAL and backward BNE/BLT/BLTU fetch policy unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add cold-start, trained forward-taken, alias, false-taken recovery, dmem-stalled training, and RVFI PC/order tests for the decode-late direction override.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 343.97, \"delta_pct\": -11.98, \"vs_baseline\": 21.62, \"fmax_mhz\": 138.41, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4023945, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10098, \"ff\": 1939, \"seeds\": [134.88, 148.15, 138.41], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:03:48.171147Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 (regression, -11.98%): Moving a one-bit direction table to ID avoids fetch lookup but its PC-indexed hint, predicted-successor override, and EX/ID redirect arbitration still cost more than CoreMark gains from correcting the remaining forward/range branches after static BNE/BLT/BLTU loop prediction.\"}\n29:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Promote SUB to ALU result class\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The latest class-partitioned ALU raised Fmax substantially, but the common\\nADD class still selects ADD versus SUB through a second-level suboperation\\ncontrol. Route reports before that partition identified this registered ALU\\ncontrol cone as timing-sensitive, while CoreMark is dominated by ADDI,\\naddress generation, and AUIPC rather than subtraction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The latest class-partitioned ALU raised Fmax substantially, but the common\\nADD class still selects ADD versus SUB through a second-level suboperation\\ncontrol. Route reports before that partition identified this registered ALU\\ncontrol cone as timing-sensitive, while CoreMark is dominated by ADDI,\\naddress generation, and AUIPC rather than subtraction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Encode SUB as its own base result class and reserve the class-local\\nsuboperation field for logic, compare, and shift operations. The ADD class\\nwill then drive its adder directly, with no ADD/SUB selection below the\\nresult-class split; loads, stores, ADD/ADDI, and AUIPC retain exactly the\\nsame single-cycle arithmetic and forwarding behavior. This is a focused\\ncontinuation of the proven ALU mux partitioning, intended to shorten the\\nremaining control-to-adder path without changing pipeline depth, prediction,\\nor RV32IM semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a dedicated SUB base-result class and redefine the base local suboperation field as unused for ADD and SUB.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the SUB class only for validated SUB encodings while leaving ADD, ADDI, loads, stores, and AUIPC in the direct ADD class.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the revised base result-class control through ID/EX without changing operand capture, valid, stall, or flush behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the unchanged-width refined ALU control to execute while preserving forwarding, branch resolution, and EX/MEM timing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Make ADD and SUB separate first-level base result branches so the ADD output has no nested ADD/SUB suboperation mux; retain all other base and M results.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed ADD/SUB class-boundary vectors plus regression coverage for address generation, logic, compare, shift, LUI, and RV32M operations.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a dedicated SUB base-result class and redefine the base local suboperation field as unused for ADD and SUB.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the SUB class only for validated SUB encodings while leaving ADD, ADDI, loads, stores, and AUIPC in the direct ADD class.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the revised base result-class control through ID/EX without changing operand capture, valid, stall, or flush behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the unchanged-width refined ALU control to execute while preserving forwarding, branch resolution, and EX/MEM timing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Make ADD and SUB separate first-level base result branches so the ADD output has no nested ADD/SUB suboperation mux; retain all other base and M results.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed ADD/SUB class-boundary vectors plus regression coverage for address generation, logic, compare, shift, LUI, and RV32M operations.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 397.38, \"delta_pct\": -22.94, \"vs_baseline\": 40.51, \"fmax_mhz\": 160.67, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4043217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10490, \"ff\": 1926, \"seeds\": [160.67, 133.33, 197.43], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:22:38.210454Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 10}\n30:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Tiny direct-mapped instruction cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The performance cosimulation injects independent instruction-bus stalls on\\nevery fetched word, while CoreMark repeatedly executes a compact set of hot\\nloop bodies. The current combinational fetch path must wait for each such\\nbus acceptance, and prior queue designs added enough wide control and data\\nrouting to lose far more Fmax than their overlap recovered.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The performance cosimulation injects independent instruction-bus stalls on\\nevery fetched word, while CoreMark repeatedly executes a compact set of hot\\nloop bodies. The current combinational fetch path must wait for each such\\nbus acceptance, and prior queue designs added enough wide control and data\\nrouting to lose far more Fmax than their overlap recovered.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small 16-line, one-word-per-line direct-mapped instruction cache\\nbetween IF and the existing instruction-memory port. Each cache line holds\\na valid bit, word address tag, and instruction; a hit supplies the current PC's word\\nwithout requiring io_imemReady, while a miss retains the existing request\\nand fill-on-accepted-response behavior. Keep the present static JAL and\\nbackward-loop prediction unchanged and invalidate entries only on reset,\\nnot on redirects. This is a\\nbounded structural source of stall tolerance that avoids a FIFO's\\nenqueue/dequeue paths and should capture CoreMark's repeatedly fetched hot\\ninstructions after warm-up.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/icache.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact 16-line direct-mapped instruction cache with one word per line plus tag, valid, accepted-fill, hit, and miss-request interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use cache hits as valid fetch responses, hold a miss PC until its bus response is accepted, and preserve the existing fetch-local prediction and redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire the instruction cache between the unchanged imem ports and IF, exposing cache miss state to pipeline control without altering the core IO or RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace raw imem-ready flushing with a qualified cache-miss wait so cache hits continue to issue during external instruction-bus stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add cache cold-miss, hit-under-imem-stall, refill, redirect, and mixed dmem-stall tests checking in-order RVFI PCs, orders, and no duplicate fetch retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/icache.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact 16-line direct-mapped instruction cache with one word per line plus tag, valid, accepted-fill, hit, and miss-request interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use cache hits as valid fetch responses, hold a miss PC until its bus response is accepted, and preserve the existing fetch-local prediction and redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and wire the instruction cache between the unchanged imem ports and IF, exposing cache miss state to pipeline control without altering the core IO or RVFI boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace raw imem-ready flushing with a qualified cache-miss wait so cache hits continue to issue during external instruction-bus stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add cache cold-miss, hit-under-imem-stall, refill, redirect, and mixed dmem-stall tests checking in-order RVFI PCs, orders, and no duplicate fetch retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 293.47, \"delta_pct\": -43.09, \"vs_baseline\": 3.77, \"fmax_mhz\": 144.63, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4928248, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10798, \"ff\": 2042, \"seeds\": [144.63, 138.2, 149.1], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:18:23.384707Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (regression, -43.09%): A one-word direct-mapped IF cache can lose badly when its tag compare, hit-data mux, and miss-wait/valid gating sit on the fetch-critical path (and each fill is bubbled), outweighing CoreMark hot-loop locality and avoided instruction-bus stalls.\"}\n31:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"BNE-focused static loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current predictor is intentionally stateless because fetch-path tables,\\nreturn stacks, and decode-time corrections have all reduced Tang Nano Fmax.\\nCoreMark's O3 counted loops are commonly lowered to backward BNE back-edges,\\nwhile the less frequent signed and unsigned range predicates can introduce\\nincorrect taken predictions and recovery bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current predictor is intentionally stateless because fetch-path tables,\\nreturn stacks, and decode-time corrections have all reduced Tang Nano Fmax.\\nCoreMark's O3 counted loops are commonly lowered to backward BNE back-edges,\\nwhile the less frequent signed and unsigned range predicates can introduce\\nincorrect taken predictions and recovery bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the existing direct-JAL plus opcode-biased backward-branch policy to\\npredict only backward BNE branches taken; BLT and BLTU will fall through.\\nKeep the current parallel PC+4, JAL-target, and branch-target adders and\\nEX-stage predicted-versus-resolved recovery unchanged. This is a pure\\nfetch-local static specialization: it retains the proven high-value BNE\\nloop prediction while eliminating the two marginal predicate cases without\\nadding predictor state, an ID redirect, or a new wide next-PC mux.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Change the backward loop-continuation predicate from BNE/BLT/BLTU to BNE only, retaining direct-JAL prediction, target generation, and redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed cases showing backward BNE is predicted taken while backward BLT and BLTU remain fall-through predicted and recover correctly in EX.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Change the backward loop-continuation predicate from BNE/BLT/BLTU to BNE only, retaining direct-JAL prediction, target generation, and redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed cases showing backward BNE is predicted taken while backward BLT and BLTU remain fall-through predicted and recover correctly in EX.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 477.09, \"delta_pct\": -7.49, \"vs_baseline\": 68.69, \"fmax_mhz\": 195.54, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4098610, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10509, \"ff\": 1926, \"seeds\": [191.42, 198.89, 195.54], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T03:14:36.295354Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (regression, -7.49%): CoreMark's backward BLT/BLTU branches remain valuable static loop-continuation predictions alongside BNE, so making them fall through reintroduces more EX recovery bubbles than it avoids from mispredicted range predicates.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
